window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2015-06-2", "source": "2015年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay commenting on Albert Einstein’s remark “ I have no special talents. I am only passionately curious.” You can give an example or two to illustrate your point of view.You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "\"I have no special talents. I am only passionately curious.\" With these modest words, Albert Einstein attributes\nhis extraordinary achievements not to inborn genius but to an unquenchable curiosity. I firmly agree: a burning\ndesire to ask \"why\" is what truly powers human progress.\nCuriosity turns ordinary effort into discovery because it keeps us questioning what others take for granted.\nEinstein himself is the best proof: he was no prodigy at school, yet his childlike wonder about light and time\ndrove him to puzzle for years over problems that others ignored, and this persistence eventually reshaped\nmodern physics. On a far smaller scale, I have felt the same force. Fascinated by how mobile apps actually\nwork, I taught myself to code late at night, not for grades but simply to satisfy my curiosity, and that habit\ngradually grew into a real skill.\nTherefore, talent may open a door, but it is curiosity that pushes us through it. If we keep our sense of wonder\nalive, even ordinary people can achieve remarkable things."}, "translation": {"prompt": "汉朝是中国历史上最重要的朝代之一。汉朝统治期间有很多显著的成就。它最先向其他文化敞开大门，对外贸易兴旺。汉朝开拓的丝绸之路通向了中西亚乃至罗马。各类艺术一派繁荣，涌现了很多文学、历史、哲学巨著。公元100年中国第一部字典编纂完成，收入9000个字，提供释义并列举不同的写法。其间，科技方面也取得了很大进步，发明了纸张、水钟、日暑(sundials )以及测量地震的仪器。汉朝历经400年，但统治者的腐败最终导致了它的灭亡。", "sample": "The Han Dynasty was one of the most important dynasties in Chinese history. There were many remarkable achievements during its reign. It was the first to open its doors to other cultures, and foreign trade flourished. The Silk Road, which was opened up by the Han Dynasty, led to Central and Western Asia and even Rome. Various forms of art flourished, and many great works of literature, history and philosophy were produced. In 100 AD, China's first dictionary was completed, containing 9,000 characters, providing definitions and listing different ways of writing. During this period, science and technology also made great progress, and paper, water clocks, sundials and instruments for measuring earthquakes were invented. The Han Dynasty lasted for about 400 years, but the corruption of its rulers eventually led to its fall."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2015-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "\"That which does not kill us makes us stronger.\" But parents can't handle it when teenagers put this  __26__  into practice. Now technology has become the new field for the age-old battle between adults and their freedom-seeking kids.\nLocked indoors, unable to get on their bicycles and hang out with their friends, teens have turned to social media and their mobile phones to socialize with their peers. What they do online often  __27__  what they might otherwise do if their mobility weren't so heavily  __28__  in the age of helicopter parenting. Social media and smart-phone apps have become so popular in recent years because teens need a place to call their own. They want the freedom to  __29__  their identity and the world around them. Instead of  __30__  out, they jump online.\nAs teens have moved online, parents have projected their fears onto the Internet, imagining all the  __31__  dangers that youth might face—from  __32__  strangers to cruel peers to pictures or words that could haunt them on Google for the rest of their lives.\nRather than helping teens develop strategies for negotiating public life and the risks of  __33__  with others, fearful parents have focused on tracking, monitoring and blocking. These tactics(策略) don't help teens develop the skills they need to manage complex social situations,  __34__  risks and get help when they're in trouble. \"Protecting\" kids may feel like the right thing to do, but it  __35__  the learning that teens need to do as they come of age in a technology-soaked world.", "matchIntro": "Inequality Is Not Inevitable", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "A dangerous trend has developed over this past third of a century. A country that experienced shared growth after World WarⅡ began to tear apart, so much so that when the Great Recession hit in late 2007, one could no longer ignore the division that had come to define the American economic landscape. How did this \"shining city on a hill\" become the advanced country with the greatest level of inequality?"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Over the past year and a half, The Great Divide, a series in The New York Times, has presented a wide range of examples that undermine the notion that there are any truly fundamental laws of capitalism. The dynamics of the imperial capitalism of the 19th century needn't apply in the democracies of the 21st. We don't need to have this much inequality in America."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Our current brand of capitalism is a fake capitalism. For proof of this go back to our response to the Great Recession, where we socialized losses, even as we privatized gains. Perfect competition should drive profits to zero, at least theoretically, but we have monopolies making persistently high profits. C.E.O.s enjoy incomes that are on average 295 times that of the typical worker, a much higher ratio than in the past, without any evidence of a proportionate increase in productivity."}, {"label": "D", "text": "If it is not the cruel laws of economics that have led to America's great divide, what is it? The straightforward answer: our policies and our politics! People get tired of hearing about Scandinavian success stories, but the fact of the matter is that Sweden, Finland and Norway have all succeeded in having about as much or faster growth in per capita(人均的) incomes than the United States and with far greater equality."}, {"label": "E", "text": "So why has America chosen these inequality-enhancing policies? Part of the answer is that as World War II faded into memory, so too did the solidarity it had created. As America triumphed in the Cold War, there didn't seem to be a real competitor to our economic model. Without this international competition, we no longer had to show that our system could deliver for most of our citizens."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Ideology and interests combined viciously. Some drew the wrong lesson from the collapse of the Soviet system in 1991. The pendulum swung from much too much government there to much too little here. Corporate interests argued for getting rid of regulations, even when those regulations had done so much to protect and improve our environment, our safety, our health and the economy itself."}, {"label": "G", "text": "But this ideology was hypocritical(虚伪的). The bankers, among the strongest advocates of laissez-faire(自由放任的) economics, were only too willing to accept hundreds of billions of dollars from the government in the aid programs that have been a recurring feature of the global economy since the beginning of the Thatcher-Reagan era of \"free\" markets and deregulation."}, {"label": "H", "text": "The American political system is overrun by money. Economic inequality translates into political inequality, and political inequality yields increasing economic inequality. So corporate welfare increases as we reduce welfare for the poor. Congress maintains subsidies for rich farmers as we cut back on nutritional support for the needy. Drug companies have been given hundreds of billions of dollars as we limit Medicaid benefits. The banks that brought on the global financial crisis got billions while a tiny bit went to the homeowners and victims of the same banks' predatory(掠夺性的) lending practices. This last decision was particularly foolish. There were alternatives to throwing money at the banks and hoping it would circulate through increased lending."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Our divisions are deep. Economic and geographic segregation has immunized those at the top from the problems of those down below. Like the kings of ancient times, they have come to perceive their privileged positions essentially as a natural right."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Our economy, our democracy and our society have paid for these gross inequalities. The true test of an economy is not how much wealth its princes can accumulate in tax havens(庇护所), but how well off the typical citizen is. But average incomes are lower than they were a quarter-century ago. Growth has gone to the very, very top, whose share has almost increased four times since 1980. Money that was meant to have trickled(流淌) down has instead evaporated in the agreeable climate of the Cayman Islands."}, {"label": "K", "text": "With almost a quarter of American children younger than 5 living in poverty, and with America doing so little for its poor, the deprivations of one generation are being visited upon the next. Of course, no country has ever come close to providing complete equality of opportunity. But why is America one of the advanced countries where the life prospects of the young are most sharply determined by the income and education of their parents?"}, {"label": "L", "text": "Among the most bitter stories in The Great Divide were those that portrayed the frustrations of the young, who long to enter our shrinking middle class. Soaring tuitions and declining incomes have resulted in larger debt burdens. Those with only a high school diploma have seen their incomes decline by 13 percent over the past 35 years."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Where justice is concerned, there is also a huge divide. In the eyes of the rest of the world and a significant part of its own population, mass imprisonment has come to define America—a country, it bears repeating, with about 5 percent of the world's population but around a fourth of the world's prisoners."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Justice has become a commodity, affordable to only a few. While Wall Street executives used their expensive lawyers to ensure that their ranks were not held accountable for the misdeeds that the crisis in 2008 so graphically revealed, the banks abused our legal system to foreclose(取消赎回权) on mortgages and eject tenants, some of whom did not even owe money."}, {"label": "O", "text": "More than a half-century ago, America led the way in advocating for the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, adopted by the United Nations in 1948. Today, access to health care is among the most universally accepted rights, at least in the advanced countries. America, despite the implementation of the Affordable Care Act, is the exception. In the relief that many felt when the Supreme Court did not overturn the Affordable Care Act, the implications of the decision for Medicaid were not fully appreciated. Obamacare's objective—to ensure that all Americans have access to health care—has been blocked: 24 states have not implemented the expanded Medicaid program, which was the means by which Obamacare was supposed to deliver on its promise to some of the poorest."}, {"label": "P", "text": "We need not just a new war on poverty but a war to protect the middle class. Solutions to these problems do not have to be novel. Far from it. Making markets act like markets would be a good place to start. We must end the rent-seeking society we have gravitated toward, in which the wealthy obtain profits by manipulating the system."}, {"label": "Q", "text": "The problem of inequality is not so much a matter of technical economics. It's really a problem of practical politics. Inequality is not just about the top marginal tax rate but also about our children's access to food and the right to justice for all. If we spent more on education, health and infrastructure(基础设施), we would strengthen our economy, now and in the future."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "I'll admit I've never quite understood the obsession(难以破除的成见) surrounding genetically modified (GM) crops. To environmentalist opponents, GM foods are simply evil, an understudied, possibly harmful tool used by big agricultural businesses to control global seed markets and crush local farmers. They argue that GM foods have never delivered on their supposed promise, that money spent on GM crops would be better channeled to organic farming and that consumers should be protected with warning labels on any products that contain genetically modified ingredients. To supporters, GM crops are a key part of the effort to sustainably provide food to meet a growing global population. But more than that, supporters see the GM opposition of many environmentalists as fundamentally anti-science, no different than those who question the basics of man-made climate change.\nFor both sides, GM foods seem to act as a symbol: you're pro-agricultural business or anti-science. But science is exactly what we need more of when it comes to GM foods, which is why I was happy to see Nature devote a special series of articles to the GM food controversy. The conclusion: while GM crops haven't yet realized their initial promise and have been dominated by agricultural businesses, there is reason to continue to use and develop them to help meet the enormous challenge of sustainably feeding a growing planet.\nThat doesn't mean GM crops are perfect, or a one-size-fits-all solution to global agriculture problems. But anything that can increase farming efficiency—the amount of crops we can produce per acre of land—will be extremely useful. GM crops can and almost certainly will be part of that suite of tools, but so will traditional plant breeding, improved soil and crop management—and perhaps most important of all, better storage and transport infrastructure(基础设施), especially in the developing world. (It doesn't do much good for farmers in places like sub-Saharan Africa to produce more food if they can't get it to hungry consumers.) I'd like to see more non-industry research done on GM crops—not just because we'd worry less about bias, but also because seed companies like Monsanto and Pioneer shouldn't be the only entities working to harness genetic modification. I'd like to see GM research on less commercial crops, like corn. I don't think it's vital to label GM ingredients in food, but I also wouldn't be against it—and industry would be smart to go along with labeling, just as a way of removing fears about the technology.\nMost of all, though, I wish a tenth of the energy that's spent endlessly debating GM crops was focused on those more pressing challenges for global agriculture. There are much bigger battles to fight.", "47": "I'll admit I've never quite understood the obsession(难以破除的成见) surrounding genetically modified (GM) crops. To environmentalist opponents, GM foods are simply evil, an understudied, possibly harmful tool used by big agricultural businesses to control global seed markets and crush local farmers. They argue that GM foods have never delivered on their supposed promise, that money spent on GM crops would be better channeled to organic farming and that consumers should be protected with warning labels on any products that contain genetically modified ingredients. To supporters, GM crops are a key part of the effort to sustainably provide food to meet a growing global population. But more than that, supporters see the GM opposition of many environmentalists as fundamentally anti-science, no different than those who question the basics of man-made climate change.\nFor both sides, GM foods seem to act as a symbol: you're pro-agricultural business or anti-science. But science is exactly what we need more of when it comes to GM foods, which is why I was happy to see Nature devote a special series of articles to the GM food controversy. The conclusion: while GM crops haven't yet realized their initial promise and have been dominated by agricultural businesses, there is reason to continue to use and develop them to help meet the enormous challenge of sustainably feeding a growing planet.\nThat doesn't mean GM crops are perfect, or a one-size-fits-all solution to global agriculture problems. But anything that can increase farming efficiency—the amount of crops we can produce per acre of land—will be extremely useful. GM crops can and almost certainly will be part of that suite of tools, but so will traditional plant breeding, improved soil and crop management—and perhaps most important of all, better storage and transport infrastructure(基础设施), especially in the developing world. (It doesn't do much good for farmers in places like sub-Saharan Africa to produce more food if they can't get it to hungry consumers.) I'd like to see more non-industry research done on GM crops—not just because we'd worry less about bias, but also because seed companies like Monsanto and Pioneer shouldn't be the only entities working to harness genetic modification. I'd like to see GM research on less commercial crops, like corn. I don't think it's vital to label GM ingredients in food, but I also wouldn't be against it—and industry would be smart to go along with labeling, just as a way of removing fears about the technology.\nMost of all, though, I wish a tenth of the energy that's spent endlessly debating GM crops was focused on those more pressing challenges for global agriculture. There are much bigger battles to fight.", "48": "I'll admit I've never quite understood the obsession(难以破除的成见) surrounding genetically modified (GM) crops. To environmentalist opponents, GM foods are simply evil, an understudied, possibly harmful tool used by big agricultural businesses to control global seed markets and crush local farmers. They argue that GM foods have never delivered on their supposed promise, that money spent on GM crops would be better channeled to organic farming and that consumers should be protected with warning labels on any products that contain genetically modified ingredients. To supporters, GM crops are a key part of the effort to sustainably provide food to meet a growing global population. But more than that, supporters see the GM opposition of many environmentalists as fundamentally anti-science, no different than those who question the basics of man-made climate change.\nFor both sides, GM foods seem to act as a symbol: you're pro-agricultural business or anti-science. But science is exactly what we need more of when it comes to GM foods, which is why I was happy to see Nature devote a special series of articles to the GM food controversy. The conclusion: while GM crops haven't yet realized their initial promise and have been dominated by agricultural businesses, there is reason to continue to use and develop them to help meet the enormous challenge of sustainably feeding a growing planet.\nThat doesn't mean GM crops are perfect, or a one-size-fits-all solution to global agriculture problems. But anything that can increase farming efficiency—the amount of crops we can produce per acre of land—will be extremely useful. GM crops can and almost certainly will be part of that suite of tools, but so will traditional plant breeding, improved soil and crop management—and perhaps most important of all, better storage and transport infrastructure(基础设施), especially in the developing world. (It doesn't do much good for farmers in places like sub-Saharan Africa to produce more food if they can't get it to hungry consumers.) I'd like to see more non-industry research done on GM crops—not just because we'd worry less about bias, but also because seed companies like Monsanto and Pioneer shouldn't be the only entities working to harness genetic modification. I'd like to see GM research on less commercial crops, like corn. I don't think it's vital to label GM ingredients in food, but I also wouldn't be against it—and industry would be smart to go along with labeling, just as a way of removing fears about the technology.\nMost of all, though, I wish a tenth of the energy that's spent endlessly debating GM crops was focused on those more pressing challenges for global agriculture. There are much bigger battles to fight.", "49": "I'll admit I've never quite understood the obsession(难以破除的成见) surrounding genetically modified (GM) crops. To environmentalist opponents, GM foods are simply evil, an understudied, possibly harmful tool used by big agricultural businesses to control global seed markets and crush local farmers. They argue that GM foods have never delivered on their supposed promise, that money spent on GM crops would be better channeled to organic farming and that consumers should be protected with warning labels on any products that contain genetically modified ingredients. To supporters, GM crops are a key part of the effort to sustainably provide food to meet a growing global population. But more than that, supporters see the GM opposition of many environmentalists as fundamentally anti-science, no different than those who question the basics of man-made climate change.\nFor both sides, GM foods seem to act as a symbol: you're pro-agricultural business or anti-science. But science is exactly what we need more of when it comes to GM foods, which is why I was happy to see Nature devote a special series of articles to the GM food controversy. The conclusion: while GM crops haven't yet realized their initial promise and have been dominated by agricultural businesses, there is reason to continue to use and develop them to help meet the enormous challenge of sustainably feeding a growing planet.\nThat doesn't mean GM crops are perfect, or a one-size-fits-all solution to global agriculture problems. But anything that can increase farming efficiency—the amount of crops we can produce per acre of land—will be extremely useful. GM crops can and almost certainly will be part of that suite of tools, but so will traditional plant breeding, improved soil and crop management—and perhaps most important of all, better storage and transport infrastructure(基础设施), especially in the developing world. (It doesn't do much good for farmers in places like sub-Saharan Africa to produce more food if they can't get it to hungry consumers.) I'd like to see more non-industry research done on GM crops—not just because we'd worry less about bias, but also because seed companies like Monsanto and Pioneer shouldn't be the only entities working to harness genetic modification. I'd like to see GM research on less commercial crops, like corn. I don't think it's vital to label GM ingredients in food, but I also wouldn't be against it—and industry would be smart to go along with labeling, just as a way of removing fears about the technology.\nMost of all, though, I wish a tenth of the energy that's spent endlessly debating GM crops was focused on those more pressing challenges for global agriculture. There are much bigger battles to fight.", "50": "I'll admit I've never quite understood the obsession(难以破除的成见) surrounding genetically modified (GM) crops. To environmentalist opponents, GM foods are simply evil, an understudied, possibly harmful tool used by big agricultural businesses to control global seed markets and crush local farmers. They argue that GM foods have never delivered on their supposed promise, that money spent on GM crops would be better channeled to organic farming and that consumers should be protected with warning labels on any products that contain genetically modified ingredients. To supporters, GM crops are a key part of the effort to sustainably provide food to meet a growing global population. But more than that, supporters see the GM opposition of many environmentalists as fundamentally anti-science, no different than those who question the basics of man-made climate change.\nFor both sides, GM foods seem to act as a symbol: you're pro-agricultural business or anti-science. But science is exactly what we need more of when it comes to GM foods, which is why I was happy to see Nature devote a special series of articles to the GM food controversy. The conclusion: while GM crops haven't yet realized their initial promise and have been dominated by agricultural businesses, there is reason to continue to use and develop them to help meet the enormous challenge of sustainably feeding a growing planet.\nThat doesn't mean GM crops are perfect, or a one-size-fits-all solution to global agriculture problems. But anything that can increase farming efficiency—the amount of crops we can produce per acre of land—will be extremely useful. GM crops can and almost certainly will be part of that suite of tools, but so will traditional plant breeding, improved soil and crop management—and perhaps most important of all, better storage and transport infrastructure(基础设施), especially in the developing world. (It doesn't do much good for farmers in places like sub-Saharan Africa to produce more food if they can't get it to hungry consumers.) I'd like to see more non-industry research done on GM crops—not just because we'd worry less about bias, but also because seed companies like Monsanto and Pioneer shouldn't be the only entities working to harness genetic modification. I'd like to see GM research on less commercial crops, like corn. I don't think it's vital to label GM ingredients in food, but I also wouldn't be against it—and industry would be smart to go along with labeling, just as a way of removing fears about the technology.\nMost of all, though, I wish a tenth of the energy that's spent endlessly debating GM crops was focused on those more pressing challenges for global agriculture. There are much bigger battles to fight.", "51": "Early decision—you apply to one school? and admission is binding—seems like a great choice for nervous applicants. Schools let in a higher percentage of early-decision applicants, which arguably means that you have a better chance of getting in. And if you do, you're done with the whole agonizing process by December. But what most students and parents don't realize is that schools have hidden motives for offering early decision.\nEarly decision, since it's binding, allows schools to fill their classes with qualified students; it allows admissions committees to select the students that are in particular demand for their college and know those students will come. It also gives schools a higher yield rate, which is often used as one of the ways to measure college selectivity and popularity.\nThe problem is that this process effectively shortens the window of time students have to make one of the most important decisions of their lives up to that point. Under regular admissions, seniors have until May 1 to choose which school to attend; early decision effectively steals six months from them, months that could be used to visit more schools, do more research, speak to current students and alumni(校友) and arguably make a more informed decision.\nThere are, frankly, an astonishing number of exceptional colleges in America, and for any given student, there are a number of schools that are a great fit. When students become too fixated(专注) on a particular school early in the admissions process, that fixation can lead to severe disappointment if they don't get in or, if they do, the possibility that they are now bound to go to a school that, given time for further reflection, may not actually be right for them.\nInsofar as early decision offers a genuine admissions edge, that advantage goes largely to students who already have numerous advantages. The students who use early decision tend to be those who have received higher-quality college guidance, usually a result of coming from a more privileged background. In this regard, there's an argument against early decision, as students from lower-income families are far less likely to have the admissions know-how to navigate the often confusing early deadlines.\nStudents who have done their research and are confident that there's one school they would be thrilled to get into should, under the current system, probably apply under early decision. But for students who haven't yet done enough research, or who are still constantly changing their minds on favorite schools, the early-decision system needlessly and prematurely narrows the field of possibility just at a time when students should be opening themselves to a whole range of thrilling options.", "52": "Early decision—you apply to one school? and admission is binding—seems like a great choice for nervous applicants. Schools let in a higher percentage of early-decision applicants, which arguably means that you have a better chance of getting in. And if you do, you're done with the whole agonizing process by December. But what most students and parents don't realize is that schools have hidden motives for offering early decision.\nEarly decision, since it's binding, allows schools to fill their classes with qualified students; it allows admissions committees to select the students that are in particular demand for their college and know those students will come. It also gives schools a higher yield rate, which is often used as one of the ways to measure college selectivity and popularity.\nThe problem is that this process effectively shortens the window of time students have to make one of the most important decisions of their lives up to that point. Under regular admissions, seniors have until May 1 to choose which school to attend; early decision effectively steals six months from them, months that could be used to visit more schools, do more research, speak to current students and alumni(校友) and arguably make a more informed decision.\nThere are, frankly, an astonishing number of exceptional colleges in America, and for any given student, there are a number of schools that are a great fit. When students become too fixated(专注) on a particular school early in the admissions process, that fixation can lead to severe disappointment if they don't get in or, if they do, the possibility that they are now bound to go to a school that, given time for further reflection, may not actually be right for them.\nInsofar as early decision offers a genuine admissions edge, that advantage goes largely to students who already have numerous advantages. The students who use early decision tend to be those who have received higher-quality college guidance, usually a result of coming from a more privileged background. In this regard, there's an argument against early decision, as students from lower-income families are far less likely to have the admissions know-how to navigate the often confusing early deadlines.\nStudents who have done their research and are confident that there's one school they would be thrilled to get into should, under the current system, probably apply under early decision. But for students who haven't yet done enough research, or who are still constantly changing their minds on favorite schools, the early-decision system needlessly and prematurely narrows the field of possibility just at a time when students should be opening themselves to a whole range of thrilling options.", "53": "Early decision—you apply to one school? and admission is binding—seems like a great choice for nervous applicants. Schools let in a higher percentage of early-decision applicants, which arguably means that you have a better chance of getting in. And if you do, you're done with the whole agonizing process by December. But what most students and parents don't realize is that schools have hidden motives for offering early decision.\nEarly decision, since it's binding, allows schools to fill their classes with qualified students; it allows admissions committees to select the students that are in particular demand for their college and know those students will come. It also gives schools a higher yield rate, which is often used as one of the ways to measure college selectivity and popularity.\nThe problem is that this process effectively shortens the window of time students have to make one of the most important decisions of their lives up to that point. Under regular admissions, seniors have until May 1 to choose which school to attend; early decision effectively steals six months from them, months that could be used to visit more schools, do more research, speak to current students and alumni(校友) and arguably make a more informed decision.\nThere are, frankly, an astonishing number of exceptional colleges in America, and for any given student, there are a number of schools that are a great fit. When students become too fixated(专注) on a particular school early in the admissions process, that fixation can lead to severe disappointment if they don't get in or, if they do, the possibility that they are now bound to go to a school that, given time for further reflection, may not actually be right for them.\nInsofar as early decision offers a genuine admissions edge, that advantage goes largely to students who already have numerous advantages. The students who use early decision tend to be those who have received higher-quality college guidance, usually a result of coming from a more privileged background. In this regard, there's an argument against early decision, as students from lower-income families are far less likely to have the admissions know-how to navigate the often confusing early deadlines.\nStudents who have done their research and are confident that there's one school they would be thrilled to get into should, under the current system, probably apply under early decision. But for students who haven't yet done enough research, or who are still constantly changing their minds on favorite schools, the early-decision system needlessly and prematurely narrows the field of possibility just at a time when students should be opening themselves to a whole range of thrilling options.", "54": "Early decision—you apply to one school? and admission is binding—seems like a great choice for nervous applicants. Schools let in a higher percentage of early-decision applicants, which arguably means that you have a better chance of getting in. And if you do, you're done with the whole agonizing process by December. But what most students and parents don't realize is that schools have hidden motives for offering early decision.\nEarly decision, since it's binding, allows schools to fill their classes with qualified students; it allows admissions committees to select the students that are in particular demand for their college and know those students will come. It also gives schools a higher yield rate, which is often used as one of the ways to measure college selectivity and popularity.\nThe problem is that this process effectively shortens the window of time students have to make one of the most important decisions of their lives up to that point. Under regular admissions, seniors have until May 1 to choose which school to attend; early decision effectively steals six months from them, months that could be used to visit more schools, do more research, speak to current students and alumni(校友) and arguably make a more informed decision.\nThere are, frankly, an astonishing number of exceptional colleges in America, and for any given student, there are a number of schools that are a great fit. When students become too fixated(专注) on a particular school early in the admissions process, that fixation can lead to severe disappointment if they don't get in or, if they do, the possibility that they are now bound to go to a school that, given time for further reflection, may not actually be right for them.\nInsofar as early decision offers a genuine admissions edge, that advantage goes largely to students who already have numerous advantages. The students who use early decision tend to be those who have received higher-quality college guidance, usually a result of coming from a more privileged background. In this regard, there's an argument against early decision, as students from lower-income families are far less likely to have the admissions know-how to navigate the often confusing early deadlines.\nStudents who have done their research and are confident that there's one school they would be thrilled to get into should, under the current system, probably apply under early decision. But for students who haven't yet done enough research, or who are still constantly changing their minds on favorite schools, the early-decision system needlessly and prematurely narrows the field of possibility just at a time when students should be opening themselves to a whole range of thrilling options.", "55": "Early decision—you apply to one school? and admission is binding—seems like a great choice for nervous applicants. Schools let in a higher percentage of early-decision applicants, which arguably means that you have a better chance of getting in. And if you do, you're done with the whole agonizing process by December. But what most students and parents don't realize is that schools have hidden motives for offering early decision.\nEarly decision, since it's binding, allows schools to fill their classes with qualified students; it allows admissions committees to select the students that are in particular demand for their college and know those students will come. It also gives schools a higher yield rate, which is often used as one of the ways to measure college selectivity and popularity.\nThe problem is that this process effectively shortens the window of time students have to make one of the most important decisions of their lives up to that point. Under regular admissions, seniors have until May 1 to choose which school to attend; early decision effectively steals six months from them, months that could be used to visit more schools, do more research, speak to current students and alumni(校友) and arguably make a more informed decision.\nThere are, frankly, an astonishing number of exceptional colleges in America, and for any given student, there are a number of schools that are a great fit. When students become too fixated(专注) on a particular school early in the admissions process, that fixation can lead to severe disappointment if they don't get in or, if they do, the possibility that they are now bound to go to a school that, given time for further reflection, may not actually be right for them.\nInsofar as early decision offers a genuine admissions edge, that advantage goes largely to students who already have numerous advantages. The students who use early decision tend to be those who have received higher-quality college guidance, usually a result of coming from a more privileged background. In this regard, there's an argument against early decision, as students from lower-income families are far less likely to have the admissions know-how to navigate the often confusing early deadlines.\nStudents who have done their research and are confident that there's one school they would be thrilled to get into should, under the current system, probably apply under early decision. But for students who haven't yet done enough research, or who are still constantly changing their minds on favorite schools, the early-decision system needlessly and prematurely narrows the field of possibility just at a time when students should be opening themselves to a whole range of thrilling options."}, "questions": [{"id": "2015-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What kind of business is the man engaged in?", "options": ["A) Providing aid to the disabled.", "B) Selling products made for left-handers.", "C) Promoting products for manufacturers.", "D) Printing labels for manufactured goods."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Selling products made for left-handers.\n【定位】Mr. Gruby, being left-handed himself, thought of the idea to try and promote a few products\nfor left-handers.\nGruby 先生本人是左撇子，想到了尝试推广一些左撇子用品的主意。\n【信号】被问者首句应答即点业务。女士开口问生意的由来，男士的回答第一段就点出做的是\nproducts for left-handers，被问者的直接应答就是答案落点；后文 About 75 percent of\nour stock is specially made for us. 进一步坐实卖的是左撇子用品。\n【替换】promote a few products for left-handers ↔ Selling products made for left-handers\n推广卖货同指经营左撇子用品\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nProviding aid to the disabled. 把服务对象改成 the disabled（残疾人）。录音通篇说的\n是 left-handers（左撇子），左撇子并非残疾人，这是偷换服务对象。\nC) 原词陷阱\nPromoting products for manufacturers. 盗用了录音原词 promote，听到 promote 就想\n选正中陷阱。但原文是 promote a few products for left-handers——为左撇子推广，而\n非 for manufacturers（替厂商推广）；后文厂商是替他生产的一方，方向被调反了。\n借用原文：promote a few products for left-handers\nD) 原词陷阱\nPrinting labels for manufactured goods. 说印标签。录音里只提到 Gruby 曾是 retired\nprinter（退休印刷工），D 蹭了 print 这个词面，但他做的生意是卖左撇子用品、不是印标\n签，印刷只是他的旧职业。\n借用原文：retired printer", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man say about his stock of products?", "options": ["A) About half of them are unavailable on the market.", "B) The kitchenware in his shop is of unique design.", "C) All of them are manufactured in his own plant.", "D) Most of them are specially made for his shop."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Most of them are specially made for his shop.\n【定位】Yes, very much so. About 75 percent of our stock is specially made for us.\n是的，很大程度上是这样。我们大约 75% 的存货是专门为我们特制的。\n【信号】数字＋stock关键词。这句同时砸出 About 75 percent 这个百分比和题干关键词 stock，\n数字本身就是答案信号；预读时已提醒「听到数字立刻记」，此处正好兑现。\n【替换】About 75 percent of our stock ↔ Most of them\n75%＝Most\nspecially made for us ↔ specially made for his shop\nfor us＝for his shop\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nAbout half of them are unavailable on the market. 既把比例从 75% 篡改成 half，又说\n成「市面上买不到」。录音 About 75 percent of our stock is specially made for us 讲的\n是特制比例，不是「买不到」的比例，比例数和说法都被改坏。\n借用原文：About 75 percent of our stock\nB) 原词陷阱\nThe kitchenware in his shop is of unique design. 提到 kitchenware（厨具），录音第\n10段确实说 a large range of kitchenware，但只说品类齐全，从未说它 unique design\n（独特设计）；B 蹭了 kitchenware 这个原词，却安上原文没有的评价。\n借用原文：a large range of kitchenware\nC) 与原文相反\nAll of them are manufactured in his own plant. 说全部由自家工厂生产。录音是\ncontacted the manufacturers with the idea of having products produced for him\n——找别的厂商代为生产，并非 his own plant（自家工厂）；且 All 与 75% 的范围也不\n符。\n借用原文：contacted the manufacturers with the idea of having products produced for him", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What can we know about the business of left-handed products?", "options": ["A) It is the man who is talking that set up the business.", "B) There was more competition in the business about 30 years ago.", "C) There are more left-handed products on the market now.", "D) People in the business should run stores to sell products."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） There are more left-handed products on the market now.\n【定位】the rest of it now, some 25, 30 years after Mr. Gruby's initial efforts, there are more left-\nhanded products actually on the market.\n剩下的那部分，如今，在 Gruby 先生最初创业约 25、30 年之后，市面上确实出现了更多\n的左撇子产品。\n【信号】now＋more的今昔对比。这句里 now 与 more left-handed products actually on the\nmarket 一起出现，正是预读盯的「今昔变化」信号词；后一句 Manufacturers are now\nbeginning to see that there is a market 再次用 now beginning 强化「现在才变多」的\n方向。\n【替换】there are more left-handed products actually on the market ↔\nThere are more left-handed products on the market now\n几乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nIt is the man who is talking that set up the business. 说创业的是正在说话的这个人。录\n音第2段是 the original shop was opened by a retired printer by the name of Gruby\n——创店人是 Gruby，不是受访的这位老板，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the original shop was opened by a retired printer by the name of Gruby\nB) 与原文相反\nThere was more competition in the business about 30 years ago. 说 30 年前竞争更\n多。录音里 30 年只用来标记时间 some 25, 30 years after Mr. Gruby's initial efforts，并\n说现在产品和市场都在变多，竞争是现在才出现，B 把方向颠倒了（说成过去更多）。\n借用原文：some 25, 30 years after Mr. Gruby's initial efforts\nD) 未提及\nPeople in the business should run stores to sell products. 说同行都应该开实体店卖\n货。录音第12段只说别家 only as mail order outlets（只做邮购）、自己才有实体店，从\n未说「同行应当开店」，这是原文没有的主张。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say about other people in his line of business?", "options": ["A) They specialise in one product only.", "B) They have outlets throughout Britain.", "C) They run chain stores in central London.", "D) They sell by mail order only."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They sell by mail order only.\n【定位】There are other people in the business now as specialists, but only as mail order outlets. But\nwe have a shop here in central London plus a mail order outlet.\n现在这行里也有别的专业经营者，但他们只做邮购。而我们在伦敦市中心有一家实体店，外\n加邮购业务。\n【信号】other people＋but切换主体。句首 other people in the business 点出主语是同行，紧\n接 but only as mail order outlets 用 but+only 限定他们的经营方式；后面再用 But we\nhave a shop 把话题切回自己——两个 but 把「别人」和「我们」清楚隔开，问 other\npeople 就只取前半。\n【替换】but only as mail order outlets ↔ They sell by mail order only\nonly mail order outlets＝by mail order only\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey specialise in one product only. 说同行只专做一种产品。录音 other people in the\nbusiness now as specialists 只说他们是 specialists（专业经营者），并未说只卖 one\nproduct；A 蹭了 specialist 这个词，却把「专业」歪解成「只做一种」。\n借用原文：other people in the business now as specialists\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey have outlets throughout Britain. 说同行门店遍布全英。录音里 outlets 只出现在\nonly as mail order outlets（邮购渠道），既没说遍布全英，outlets 指的也是邮购点而非\n实体连锁店，B 盗用 outlets 一词又夸大了范围。\n借用原文：only as mail order outlets\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey run chain stores in central London. 把伦敦市中心的店安到同行（They）头上。但\nBut we have a shop here in central London 明说在伦敦有店的是「我们」、且只是 a\nshop（一家店）不是 chain stores（连锁）；C 既张冠李戴又夸大成连锁。\n借用原文：But we have a shop here in central London", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about the man's company?", "options": ["A) It publishes magazines.", "B) It is engaged in product design.", "C) It sponsors trade fairs.", "D) It runs sales promotion campaigns."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It runs sales promotion campaigns.\n【定位】M: Can we make you an offer? We would like to run the campaign for four extra weeks.\n男：我们能给您提个方案吗？我们想把这次推广活动再多做四周。\n【信号】男方亲口说\"我们来做推广\"，自述业务。答案信号是男方的自述句 We would like to run\nthe campaign for four extra weeks.——他张口就提出\"由我方再跑四周推广活动\"，说明\n经营推广是他公司的本职业务；这是说话人自报家门式的应答，不是靠关键词扫描得来。\n【替换】run the campaign ↔ runs sales promotion campaigns\n原文动作 run the campaign 概括成业务名 promotion campaigns\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第 2 段女方提到 The ads also did not appear in two key magazines. 念到了\nmagazines（杂志），但那只是说广告没登上杂志，绝不等于男方公司\"出版杂志\"；选项 It\npublishes magazines. 盗用这个原词诱你误选。\n借用原文：The ads also did not appear in two key magazines.\nB) 未提及\nIt is engaged in product design. 的\"产品设计\"在整段对话里根本没出现，纯属凭\"商务公\n司\"的常识硬猜，属无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第 2 段女方说 It missed two important trade fairs. 念到了 trade fairs（展会），但\n那是\"推广错过了展会\"，而非男方公司\"赞助展会\"；选项 It sponsors trade fairs. 盗用这个\n原词，把\"错过\"偷换成\"赞助\"。\n借用原文：It missed two important trade fairs.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "Why was the campaign delayed according to the man?", "options": ["A) The woman's company failed to make payments in time.", "B) The ad specifications had not been given in detail.", "C) The woman's company made last-minute changes.", "D) Organising the promotion was really time-consuming."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The woman's company made last-minute changes.\n【定位】M: Well, the delay wasn't entirely our fault. You did in fact make late changes to the\nspecifications of the advertisements.\n男：嗯，这次延误不全是我们的责任。事实上是你们对广告的规格说明做了临时改动。\n【信号】题干限定 the man，定位男方的甩锅应答句。答案信号是男方把责任推回去的应答 You did\nin fact make late changes to the specifications of the advertisements.——他用\"不全\n怪我们\"转而指出对方临时改了规格。这正是 according to the man 要的那句，靠\"被问者/\n男方的应答\"信号锁定，不是扫关键词。\n【替换】You did in fact make late changes to the specifications of the advertisements. ↔\nThe woman's company made last-minute changes.\nyou→the woman's company，late changes→last-minute changes\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThe woman's company failed to make payments in time. 说的\"未按时付款\"，对话里压\n根没提付款这回事，属无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音第 3 段男方念到 the specifications of the advertisements（广告的规格说明），选\n项 The ad specifications had not been given in detail. 盗用 specifications，编造\"规格\n说明没给详细\"——而男方的原意是\"规格被临时改动\"，不是\"没写详细\"。\n借用原文：make late changes to the specifications of the advertisements\nD) 答非所问\nOrganising the promotion was really time-consuming. 说\"组织推广很费时\"，这既不是\n男方给出的延误原因，录音也没这么说；男方明确把原因归到女方改规格，与\"本身就费\n时\"无关。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the woman propose as a solution to the problem?", "options": ["A) Run another four-week campaign.", "B) Give her a 10 percent discount.", "C) Extend the campaign to next year.", "D) Cut the fee by half for this year."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Cut the fee by half for this year.\n【定位】W: That's not really the point. The campaign missed two key trade fairs. Because of this, we\nare asking you either to repeat the campaign next year for free, or we only pay 50% of the fee\nfor this year.\n女：那不是重点。这次推广错过了两场关键展会。因此，我们要求你们要么明年免费重做一\n次推广，要么我们今年只付一半的费用。\n【信号】女方的 we are asking you 提议句，含 either...or 双方案。答案信号是女方亲口提议的\nwe are asking you either to repeat the campaign next year for free, or we only pay\n50% of the fee for this year——we are asking 就是\"提议\"的应答动作，either...or 摆出\n两个方案。这是女方的提议句，靠\"提议动词\"信号锁定。\n【替换】we only pay 50% of the fee for this year ↔ Cut the fee by half for this year\n只付 50% 的费用 = 把费用砍掉一半\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nRun another four-week campaign. 的\"再做四周推广\"来自男方第 1、5 段的 run the\ncampaign for four extra weeks，是男方的提议，不是女方的；张冠李戴。\n借用原文：run the campaign for four extra weeks\nB) 未提及\nGive her a 10 percent discount. 的\"10% 折扣\"在对话里根本没出现（男方提的是 20% 减\n让），属把数字改错的无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里女方说的是 repeat the campaign next year for free（明年免费重做），选项\nExtend the campaign to next year. 盗用 next year，却把\"免费重做\"偷换成\"把活动延长\n到明年\"，意思不同。\n借用原文：repeat the campaign next year for free", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the man suggest they do at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) Calm down and make peace.", "B) Improve their promotion plans.", "C) Stop negotiating for the time being.", "D) Reflect on their respective mistakes."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Stop negotiating for the time being.\n【定位】M: Well, let's take a break. We're not getting very far. Perhaps we should think about this.\n男：好吧，我们先休息一下。我们没什么进展。也许我们该再想想这事。\n【信号】录音末段男方的 let's take a break 建议句。答案信号是对话最后一段男方的提议 let's\ntake a break 加 Perhaps we should think about this.——这是结尾处的\"建议\"应答句，\nlet's/Perhaps we should 都是提议语气，正是 at the end 要的那句。\n【替换】let's take a break ↔ Stop negotiating for the time being\n暂时休息=暂时停止谈判\nWe're not getting very far. ↔ Stop negotiating for the time being\n\"没进展\"佐证是暂停谈判而非别的动作\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nCalm down and make peace. 的\"冷静下来、握手言和\"听着像谈判收尾的暖话，但男方结\n尾只说休息、再想想，没提\"和好\"，属凭情景想象的过度推断。\nB) 原词陷阱\nImprove their promotion plans. 的 promotion 来自前文谈的推广活动，但男方结尾说的\n是\"休息、再想想\"，没说要\"改进推广方案\"；盗用 promotion 制造熟悉感。\n借用原文：The campaign missed two key trade fairs.\nD) 原词陷阱\nReflect on their respective mistakes. 的\"各自反省错误\"借用了男方第 9 段 I think we\nboth made mistakes. 里的 mistakes，但那是男方早先承认\"双方都有错\"，并非结尾 at\nthe end 的建议；结尾男方建议的是\"休息、再想\"，不是\"反省错误\"。\n借用原文：I think we both made mistakes.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the passage say about most of the mice used for experiments?", "options": ["A) They are labeled pet animals by the researchers.", "B) They look spotlessly clean throughout their lives.", "C) They are looked after by animal-care organizations.", "D) They sacrifice their lives for the benefit of humans."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They sacrifice their lives for the benefit of humans.\n【定位】Of the 15,000 mice housed there in a typical year, most give their lives for humanity.\n在那里每年圈养的大约15,000只老鼠中，大多数都为人类献出了生命。\n【信号】most＋概括句。信号是题干里的 most 直接对应原文 most give their lives for humanity\n这句带 most 的概括陈述——录音里出现和题干同一限定词（most）的概括句，往往就是\n答案句，不必去抠前后的细节。\n【替换】most give their lives for humanity ↔ They sacrifice their lives for the benefit of humans.\ngive lives＝sacrifice lives 同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实反复讲 the labels we put on things，选项 A 用 labeled pet animals by the\nresearchers 蹭这个原词，但原文说的是“贴标签会影响道德判断”，从未说研究者把大多数\n老鼠标成 pet（宠物），听到 label 就选 A 正是原词陷阱。\n借用原文：the labels we put on things can affect our moral responses to them\nB) 张冠李戴\n原文 a model animal facility, spotlessly clean 里的 spotlessly clean 形容的是“实验楼这\n个设施”干净，B 项 They look spotlessly clean throughout their lives. 把它安到“老鼠身\n上、且终生干净”，把形容设施的词挪给了老鼠，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a model animal facility, spotlessly clean\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文出现 won the protection of the animal-care committee，是说“好老鼠赢得动物照护\n委员会的保护”，C 项 They are looked after by animal-care organizations. 把 animal-\ncare 这个原词留下，却说成“被动物照护组织照料”，且原文只针对“好老鼠”而非 most 老\n鼠，既偷换了主体范围又夸大了照护，故排。\n借用原文：These are \"good\" mice and as such won the protection of the animal-care committee.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "Why did the so-called bad mice have to be captured and destroyed?", "options": ["A) They may behave abnormally.", "B) They may breed out of control.", "C) They may affect the results of experiments.", "D) They may cause damage to the environment."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They may affect the results of experiments.\n【定位】These mice are \"pests\". They can disrupt experiments with the bacteria organisms they carry.\n这些老鼠是“害兽”。它们身上携带的细菌生物会扰乱实验。\n【信号】disrupt experiments＝破坏信号。信号是 disrupt experiments 这个明确的“破坏”动词\n——录音把坏老鼠和“扰乱实验”直接挂钩，正是回答 Why 的因果落点，听到 disrupt 就该\n锁定这句。\n【替换】They can disrupt experiments with the bacteria organisms they carry ↔\nThey may affect the results of experiments.\ndisrupt experiments＝affect the results 概括改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文只说坏老鼠携带细菌扰乱实验，从没说它们 behave abnormally（行为异常）；“行为\n异常”是听者凭“坏老鼠”二字想当然补出来的，原文无据，属未提及。\nB) 未提及\nThey may breed out of control.（失控繁殖）是老鼠常见的刻板印象，但本段讲的是它们\n携带细菌扰乱实验，全程未提繁殖问题，属凭常识脑补的未提及项。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文坏老鼠的危害是对“实验”，D 项 They may cause damage to the environment. 把危\n害对象偷换成“环境”，实验室里扰乱实验和破坏环境是两码事，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：They can disrupt experiments with the bacteria organisms they carry", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "When are mice killed without prior approval?", "options": ["A) When they are no longer useful.", "B) When they become ill.", "C) When they become escapees.", "D) When they get too old."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） When they become escapees.\n【定位】Once a research animal hits the floor and becomes an escapee, says Herzog, its moral\nstanding is instantly diminished.\n赫尔佐格说，一旦一只实验动物掉到地上、变成逃跑者，它的道德地位立刻就被降低了。\n【信号】Once＋条件信号。信号是条件连词 Once——录音用 Once a research animal …\nbecomes an escapee 引出条件，紧接着说它道德地位骤降（即可不经审批处死），听到\nOnce 就该锁定这句作答 When。\n【替换】Once a research animal hits the floor and becomes an escapee ↔ When they become escapees.\nbecomes an escapee＝become escapees 近乎直取\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文触发无须审批处死的条件是“成为逃跑者”，而非 no longer useful（不再有用）；“没用\n了就处理掉”是听者对实验动物的合理联想，但原文并未以“失去用处”为条件，属未提及。\nB) 未提及\nwhen they become ill.（生病时）原文未提；坏老鼠是“携带细菌”，但那是它们害实验的原\n因，并非“生病才被无审批处死”的条件，二者不可混为一谈，属未提及。\nD) 未提及\nwhen they get too old.（太老时）同样是对实验动物命运的常识性猜测，原文从未以“年\n老”为处死条件，无出处，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why does the speaker say what the Herzogs did at home is ironical?", "options": ["A) While holding a burial ceremony for a pet mouse, they were killing pest mice.", "B) While advocating freedom for animals, they kept their pet mouse in a cage.", "C) While calling for animal rights, they allowed their kids to keep pet animals.", "D) While launching animal protection campaigns, they were trapping kitchen mice."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） While holding a burial ceremony for a pet mouse, they were killing pest mice.\n【定位】when his young son's pet mouse Willy died recently, it was accorded a tearful ceremonial\nburial in the garden. Yet even as they mourned Willy, says Herzog, he and his wife were\nsetting snap traps to kill the pest mice in their kitchen.\n最近他小儿子的宠物鼠威利死了，它在花园里得到了一场含泪的隆重葬礼。然而就在他们为\n威利哀悼的同时，赫尔佐格说，他和妻子正在厨房里布置弹簧夹来消灭害鼠。\n【信号】转折 Yet＋同时对照。信号是转折词 Yet even as they mourned Willy——Yet 把前面“为\n宠物鼠厚葬”与后面“为害鼠设夹处死”猛地对照起来，even as（就在…的同时）强化了同时\n进行的矛盾，这正是讽刺的落点。\n【替换】it was accorded a tearful ceremonial burial in the garden ↔\nWhile holding a burial ceremony for a pet mouse\nceremonial burial＝burial ceremony 同义重组\nsetting snap traps to kill the pest mice in their kitchen ↔ they were killing pest mice\nsetting traps to kill＝killing 概括改写\n【排除】B) 未提及\nWhile advocating freedom for animals, they kept their pet mouse in a cage. 里\nadvocating freedom（倡导自由）和 kept… in a cage（关进笼子）原文都没说，是借“动\n物伦理”话题编出的矛盾，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文确有 his young son's pet mouse Willy（儿子的宠物鼠），C 项 While calling for\nanimal rights, they allowed their kids to keep pet animals. 蹭“孩子/宠物”原词，却把矛\n盾说成“呼吁动物权利却允许孩子养宠物”，既非原文那组对照、calling for animal rights 也\n没说，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：when his young son's pet mouse Willy died recently\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文 setting snap traps to kill the pest mice in their kitchen 里的 traps、kitchen 全被\nD 项 While launching animal protection campaigns, they were trapping kitchen\nmice. 借走，但前半 launching animal protection campaigns（发起动物保护运动）原文\n没说——原文矛盾的前半是“厚葬宠物鼠”而非“搞动保运动”，盗了原词却换了对照的另一\n半，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：he and his wife were setting snap traps to kill the pest mice in their kitchen", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the natives of New York?", "options": ["A) They contribute most to it.", "B) They are crazy about it.", "C) They often find fault with it.", "D) They take it for granted."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They take it for granted.\n【定位】There is, first, the New York of the man or woman who was born here, who takes the city for\ngranted and accepts its size and its turbulence as natural and inevitable.\n首先，有这样一座属于土生土长者的纽约：他们把这座城市视作理所当然，把它的庞大与喧\n嚣都当成自然而然、不可避免的事。\n【信号】born here 锁定 natives 的描述句。原文虽未直接用 natives 一词，但 the man or\nwoman who was born here (生在这里的人)正是 natives，紧跟的 who takes the city for\ngranted 就是对这群人的核心刻画，听到“born here”就该锁定其后的描述。\n【替换】takes the city for granted ↔ They take it for granted.\n原句近乎原样保留\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThey contribute most to it. 听着像原文“the greatest”，但原文说贡献最大、最伟大的是\n第三类人 settlers(the greatest is the last)，不是 natives，把后者的功劳安到了 natives\n头上。\n借用原文：Of these three trembling cities the greatest is the last\nB) 张冠李戴\nThey are crazy about it. 对应的是 settlers “像初恋般热烈拥抱纽约”(each embraces New\nYork with the intense excitement of first love)，那是第三类人，不是 natives，属张冠李\n戴。\n借用原文：each embraces New York with the intense excitement of first love\nC) 未提及\nThey often find fault with it. (常挑城市毛病)在原文中找不到任何对应，natives 是“take\nfor granted”而非批评，属凭空捏造。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the speaker say commuters give to New York?", "options": ["A) Tidal restlessness.", "B) Heat and light.", "C) Economic prosperity.", "D) Historical continuity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Tidal restlessness.\n【定位】Commuters give the city its tidal restlessness; natives give it solidity and continuity; but the\nsettlers give it passion.\n通勤者给这座城市带来潮汐般的躁动；本地人给它以稳固和延续；而定居者给它以激情。\n【信号】三个 give 并列，紧跟主语取宾语。Commuters give the city its tidal restlessness 是三\n组“某群人 give it 某物”并列中的第一组；信号就是主语“Commuters”后紧跟的 give 宾语，\n听到“Commuters give…”立刻锁住其后名词。\n【替换】tidal restlessness ↔ Tidal restlessness.\n原词短语整体搬入选项\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nHeat and light. 确在原文出现，但说的是 settlers “生出热与光”(each generates heat and\nlight)，不是 commuters 给的，盗用了原词却安错了人。\n借用原文：each generates heat and light to dwarf the Consolidated Edison Company\nC) 未提及\nEconomic prosperity. (经济繁荣)在原文里根本没出现，commuters 给的是 tidal\nrestlessness 而非经济，属无中生有。\nD) 张冠李戴\nHistorical continuity. 对应原文 natives “give it solidity and continuity”(natives give it\nsolidity and continuity)，是本地人给的，不是 commuters，又一处张冠李戴。\n借用原文：natives give it solidity and continuity", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What do we learn about the settlers of New York?", "options": ["A) They lack knowledge of the culture of the city.", "B) They are adventurers from all over the world.", "C) They have difficulty surviving.", "D) They find the city alien to them."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They are adventurers from all over the world.\n【定位】each embraces New York with the intense excitement of first love; each absorbs New York\nwith the fresh eyes of an adventurer; each generates heat and light to dwarf the Consolidated\nEdison Company.\n他们每个人都怀着初恋般强烈的兴奋拥抱纽约；每个人都以冒险者新奇的眼光去吸纳纽约；\n每个人都迸发出令爱迪生电力公司都相形见绌的热与光。\n【信号】an adventurer 直接点出 settlers 身份。描写 settlers 的句子里 with the fresh eyes of\nan adventurer (以冒险者新奇的眼光)直接把这群人比作“冒险者”，这个比喻就是答案信\n号，听到“adventurer”即可锁定。\n【替换】with the fresh eyes of an adventurer ↔ They are adventurers from all over the world.\n比喻提取为身份判断\nthe person who was born somewhere else and came to New York ↔ from all over the world\n“别处来的人”概括为“来自世界各地”\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey lack knowledge of the culture of the city. 与原文相反：原文说 settlers 给纽约带来\n“对艺术的投入”与“无可比拟的成就”(its dedication to the arts, and its incomparable\nachievements)，绝非缺乏文化，属与原文相反。\n借用原文：its dedication to the arts, and its incomparable achievements\nC) 未提及\nThey have difficulty surviving. 原文未提；虽有意大利农夫开小杂货铺、姑娘逃离故乡等\n例子，但讲的是他们怀着激情而来，并非生存艰难，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey find the city alien to them. (觉得城市陌生疏离)与原文相反：settlers “像初恋般拥抱\n纽约”(each embraces New York with the intense excitement of first love)，是亲近狂热\n而非疏离。\n借用原文：each embraces New York with the intense excitement of first love", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "According to Professor Dreiser, which of the following discoveries was made during the first era in the history of nutrition?", "options": ["A) Protein was recognized as an essential component of diet.", "B) Vitamins were synthesized from foods.", "C) Effective measures of weight loss were determined.", "D) Certain foods were found to be harmful to good health."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Protein was recognized as an essential component of diet.\n【定位】The first era began in the nineteenth century and extended into the early twentieth century\nwhen it was recognized for the first time that food contained components that were essential\nfor human function... rapid weight loss was associated with nutrition imbalance and could\nonly be rectified by providing adequate dietary protein associated with certain foods.\n第一个时期始于十九世纪、延续到二十世纪初，那时人们首次认识到食物含有对人体机能至\n关重要的成分……快速的体重下降与营养失衡有关，只能靠提供与某些食物相关的足量膳食\n蛋白质来纠正。\n【信号】首次认识到 + 阶段标志词。答案句的信号是阶段标志词加\"首次认识\"：录音先用 The first\nera 点出正是题目要问的第一时期，随后 it was recognized for the first time that food\ncontained components that were essential for human function 用\"首次认识到……必需\n成分\"正面给出该时期的发现，这句就是答案句。\n【替换】food contained components that were essential for human function ↔\nProtein was recognized as an essential component of diet\n概括 + 蛋白质点名\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nVitamins were synthesized from foods 盗用了录音里的高频词 vitamins，但维生素是\nThe second era... might be called the vitamin period 第二时期的事，不是第一时期；且\n录音说维生素是\"在食物中被认识\"，不是\"被合成\"。听到 vitamins 就选，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：The second era was initiated in the early decades of the twentieth century and might be\ncalled the vitamin period\nC) 原词陷阱\nEffective measures of weight loss were determined 借用了录音原词 rapid weight\nloss，但录音说的是\"快速减重与营养失衡有关、需靠蛋白质纠正\"，是把减重当\"问题\"来纠\n正，绝非\"确定了有效的减重方法\"。盗 weight loss 一词却把意思整个说反。\n借用原文：rapid weight loss was associated with nutrition imbalance and could only be rectified\nby providing adequate dietary protein\nD) 未提及\nCertain foods were found to be harmful to good health 听起来像那么回事，但录音第\n一时期只讲\"食物含必需成分\"\"蛋白质能纠正失衡\"，从没说\"某些食物有害健康\"。这是录音\n根本没出现的内容。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is the purpose of teaching concepts of nutrition in medical schools?", "options": ["A) In order to urge medical doctors to participate in research studies on nutrition.", "B) In order to encourage medical doctors to apply concepts of nutrition in the treatment of disease.", "C) In order to persuade doctors to conduct experimental vitamin therapies on their patients.", "D) In order to support the creation of artificial vitamins."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题）\nIn order to encourage medical doctors to apply concepts of nutrition in the treatment of\ndisease.\n【定位】As vitamins became recognized as essential food constituents necessary for health, it became\ntempting to suggest that every disease and condition for which there had been no previous\neffective treatment might be responsive to vitamin therapy. At that point in time, medical\nschools started to become more interested in having their curricula integrate nutritional\nconcepts into the basic sciences.\n随着维生素被认识为对健康必需的食物成分，人们很想提出：以往没有有效疗法的每一种疾\n病和病症，或许都能对维生素疗法有反应。正是在那个时候，医学院开始更有兴趣把营养概\n念整合进基础科学课程。\n【信号】因果衔接 At that point。答案句的信号是前后因果衔接：录音先说大家倾向于认为 every\ndisease and condition... might be responsive to vitamin therapy（疾病或可用维生素疗\n法应对），紧接 At that point in time, medical schools started to become more\ninterested in having their curricula integrate nutritional concepts——医学院教营养，\n正是承接\"把营养用于治病\"这股潮流而来，目的就藏在这层因果里。\n【替换】might be responsive to vitamin therapy ↔\napply concepts of nutrition in the treatment of disease\n用维生素治病 = 营养用于治疗\nintegrate nutritional concepts into the basic sciences ↔\nencourage medical doctors to apply concepts of nutrition\n课程整合 → 鼓励应用\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nurge medical doctors to participate in research studies on nutrition 借了录音常出现的\nresearch / nutrition 词面，但录音说医学院\"把营养整合进课程\"是为让医生临床上用营养治\n病，不是号召医生去\"参与营养研究\"。教学目的被偷换成科研目的。\n借用原文：medical schools started to become more interested in having their curricula integrate\nnutritional concepts into the basic sciences\nC) 过度推断\nconduct experimental vitamin therapies on their patients 盗用了 vitamin therapy 一\n词，并把它夸大成\"在病人身上做实验性疗法\"。录音只说设想疾病\"或可对维生素疗法有反\n应\"，从未说要让医生在患者身上做实验，这是把语气加重的过度推断。\n借用原文：every disease and condition for which there had been no previous effective treatment\nmight be responsive to vitamin therapy\nD) 未提及\nsupport the creation of artificial vitamins 蹭了 vitamins 一词，但录音讲医学院教营养是\n为临床应用，全程没提\"支持制造人工维生素\"。这是录音未出现的内容，与教学目的无关。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "Why did vitamin therapy become less popular in the 1950s?", "options": ["A) The public lost interest in vitamins.", "B) Medical schools stopped teaching nutritional concepts.", "C) Nutritional research was of poor quality.", "D) Claims for the effectiveness of vitamin therapy were exaggerated."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Claims for the effectiveness of vitamin therapy were exaggerated.\n【定位】In the third era of nutritional history in the early 1950s to mid-1960s, vitamin therapy\nbegan to fall into disrepute... Expectations as to the success of vitamins in disease control\nwere exaggerated.\n在 1950 年代初到 60 年代中期的营养学第三个时期，维生素疗法开始名声扫地……人们对维\n生素在疾病控制中成效的期望被夸大了。\n【信号】负面措辞 + 夸大表述。答案句的信号有两层：先是负面措辞 vitamin therapy began to\nfall into disrepute（开始失宠）点明现象，随后 Expectations as to the success of\nvitamins in disease control were exaggerated 用\"成效期望被夸大\"正面给出原因。听\n到\"失宠\"后紧跟的 exaggerated 就是答案落点。\n【替换】Expectations as to the success of vitamins in disease control were exaggerated ↔\nClaims for the effectiveness of vitamin therapy were exaggerated\n近乎同义对译\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThe public lost interest in vitamins 听着像，但录音说变得不那么受欢迎的是 nutrition\neducation in medical schools also became less popular——是医学院的营养教育，且\ndrug companies had found their vitamin sales skyrocketing 显示销量曾猛涨，公众并\n未失去兴趣。把\"医学院\"张冠李戴成\"公众\"。\n借用原文：nutrition education in medical schools also became less popular\nB) 原词陷阱\nMedical schools stopped teaching nutritional concepts 盗用了 medical schools、\nnutritional concepts 等原词，但录音只说医学院营养教育\"became less popular\"（变得\n不那么受欢迎），不是 stopped（停止教）。把\"变冷门\"夸大成\"停教\"，程度说过了。\n借用原文：nutrition education in medical schools also became less popular\nC) 未提及\nNutritional research was of poor quality 蹭了 nutrition 词面，但录音从未说\"营养研究\n质量差\"，只说对维生素\"成效的期望被夸大\"。把原因偷换成\"研究质量低\"，是录音没说的内\n容。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the talk mainly discuss?", "options": ["A) How babies differentiate between the sound of the human voice and other sounds.", "B) The differences between a baby's and an adult's ability to comprehend language.", "C) How babies perceive and respond to the human voice in their early stages of language development.", "D) The response of a baby to sounds other than the human voice."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\nHow babies perceive and respond to the human voice in their early stages of language\ndevelopment.\n【定位】Long before they can actually speak, babies pay special attention to the speech they hear\naround them. Within the first month of their lives, babies' responses to the sound of the\nhuman voice will be different from their responses to other sorts of sounds.\n早在真正会说话之前，婴儿就会特别留意周围听到的话语。在出生头一个月内，婴儿对人声\n的反应就会不同于对其他各种声音的反应。\n【信号】开篇点题句统领全文。讲座第一句 babies pay special attention to the speech they hear\naround them 即抛出主题，后文从\"区分人声\"\"母亲的说话方式\"\"分辨语音\"\"享受声音\"四个\n方面层层展开，都是对它的细化，因此开篇这句就是主旨落点。\n【替换】pay special attention to the speech ↔ perceive and respond to the human voice\npay attention to ≈ perceive and respond to\nLong before they can actually speak ↔ in their early stages of language development\n会说话之前 ≈ 语言发展早期\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nHow babies differentiate between the sound of the human voice and other sounds.\n说的\"区分人声与其他声音\"，确实在 babies' responses to the sound of the human\nvoice will be different from their responses to other sorts of sounds 出现过，但那只\n是开篇举的第一个表现，全文还讲了母亲说话、分辨语音、享受声音，A 只占一段，撑不起\n主旨。\n借用原文：babies' responses to the sound of the human voice will be different from their responses\nto other sorts of sounds\nB) 未提及\nThe differences between a baby's and an adult's ability to comprehend language. 把\n主题说成\"婴儿与成人理解能力之差\"，但全文聚焦婴儿一方，只在结尾顺带提了一句成人\n（the route to dull meaning that it often is for adults），从未以\"婴儿 vs 成人对比\"为\n主线。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe response of a baby to sounds other than the human voice. 把主题说成\"对非人声\n的反应\"，而全文恰恰强调婴儿对\"人声\"的特别关注，非人声（如 a bell ring）只是用来反\n衬人声的配角，D 把主次彻底颠倒。\n借用原文：they hear a bell ring", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "Why does the speaker mention the ringing of a bell?", "options": ["A) To contrast the reactions of babies to human and nonhuman sounds.", "B) To give examples of sounds that will cause a baby to cry.", "C) To explain how babies distinguish between different nonhuman sounds.", "D) To give examples of typical toys that babies do not like."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （目的题） To contrast the reactions of babies to human and nonhuman sounds.\n【定位】They will stop crying when they hear a person talking, but not if they hear a bell ring.\n婴儿听到有人说话会停止哭泣，但听到铃声却不会。\n【信号】转折对照句 but not。铃声出现在 They will stop crying when they hear a person\ntalking, but not if they hear a bell ring 这个 but 对照结构里：人声让婴儿止哭、铃声不\n能。提到铃声正是为了与人声形成反差，证明婴儿对人声有特别反应。\n【替换】stop crying when they hear a person talking, but not if they hear a bell ring ↔\ncontrast the reactions of babies to human and nonhuman sounds\nbut 对照 ≈ contrast\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nTo give examples of sounds that will cause a baby to cry. 说提铃声是举\"让婴儿哭的声\n音\"之例，但原文是 They will stop crying——讲的是\"停止哭\"，且铃声是\"不能让婴儿止\n哭\"，并非\"让婴儿哭\"，方向完全拧了。\n借用原文：They will stop crying when they hear a person talking\nC) 偷换概念\nTo explain how babies distinguish between different nonhuman sounds. 说是解释婴\n儿如何区分\"不同的非人声之间\"，但原文的对照是\"人声 vs 非人声\"，铃声只是非人声的一个\n代表，根本没讲非人声彼此之间的区分。\n借用原文：responses to the sound of the human voice will be different from their responses to\nother sorts of sounds\nD) 未提及\nTo give examples of typical toys that babies do not like. 把铃声说成\"婴儿不喜欢的玩\n具\"之例，原文只字未提 toys，更没说婴儿\"不喜欢\"，纯属编造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What can we learn from the findings of babies and their mothers in six diverse cultures?", "options": ["A) Babies who are exposed to more than one language can acquire languages earlier than those to a single language.", "B) Mothers from different cultures speak to their babies in a similar way.", "C) Babies ignore facial expressions in comprehending their parents' language.", "D) The mothers observed by the researchers were consciously teaching their babies to speak."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Mothers from different cultures speak to their babies in a similar way.\n【定位】One researcher observed babies and their mothers in six diverse cultures and found that, in all\nsix languages, the mothers used simplified grammar, short sentences, and transformed certain\nsounds into baby talk.\n一位研究者观察了六种不同文化中的婴儿及其母亲，发现在所有六种语言里，母亲都使用了\n简化的语法、短句，并把某些音转成了婴儿语。\n【信号】研究信号词 observed…found that。One researcher observed…and found that, in all\nsix languages, the mothers used simplified grammar, short sentences 里 found that\n引出的正是该研究的结论，in all six languages 强调\"六种语言里母亲做法一致\"，这就是答\n案句。\n【替换】in all six languages, the mothers used simplified grammar, short sentences ↔\nspeak to their babies in a similar way\n六种语言做法一致 ≈ 方式相似\nsix diverse cultures ↔ Mothers from different cultures\ndiverse cultures = different cultures\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nBabies who are exposed to more than one language can acquire languages earlier\nthan those to a single language. 拿\"接触多语言的婴儿习得更早\"作答，但这段研究讲的\n是\"母亲说话方式\"，从未比较多语言与单语言婴儿谁习得更早，earlier 这一比较是凭空加\n的。\nC) 与原文相反\nBabies ignore facial expressions in comprehending their parents' language. 说婴\n儿\"忽略面部表情\"，但原文 They also exaggerate their facial expressions 讲的是母亲\"夸\n大面部表情\"来帮助婴儿，方向正好相反，且主语也从母亲偷换成婴儿。\n借用原文：They also exaggerate their facial expressions\nD) 过度推断\nThe mothers observed by the researchers were consciously teaching their babies to\nspeak. 称母亲在\"有意识地教婴儿说话\"，但原文说母亲是 make it as easy as they can for\nbabies to pick up a language（尽量让婴儿好学），是自然简化语言、并非 consciously\n刻意施教，consciously 是无中生有的拔高。\n借用原文：Adults make it as easy as they can for babies to pick up a language", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "According to the talk, why do babies listen to songs and stories, even though they cannot understand them?", "options": ["A) They understand the rhythm.", "B) They enjoy the sounds of them.", "C) They can remember them easily.", "D) They focus on their parents' words."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They enjoy the sounds of them.\n【定位】Babies obviously derive pleasure from sound input, too: at even as young as nine months they\nwill listen to songs or stories, although the words themselves are beyond their understanding.\n婴儿显然也能从声音输入中获得乐趣：甚至小到九个月大，他们就会听歌或听故事，尽管那\n些词本身超出了他们的理解。\n【信号】因果/解释句 derive pleasure。Babies obviously derive pleasure from sound input,\ntoo 先给出原因——婴儿从声音中获得乐趣，冒号后才说他们\"会听歌、听故事，尽管听不\n懂词\"，所以\"听\"的原因正是\"从声音中得到快乐\"。\n【替换】Babies obviously derive pleasure from sound input ↔ They enjoy the sounds of them.\nderive pleasure from sound ≈ enjoy the sounds\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey understand the rhythm. 说婴儿\"理解节奏\"，但原文恰恰强调 the words\nthemselves are beyond their understanding（词义超出他们的理解），通篇没说他们\"理\n解\"任何东西，understand 与原文基调相悖。\n借用原文：although the words themselves are beyond their understanding\nC) 未提及\nThey can remember them easily. 说婴儿\"容易记住\"歌和故事，但原文只讲\"获得乐趣\"，\n从未提及记忆或 remember，属凭空添加。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey focus on their parents' words. 说婴儿\"专注于父母的话语（词）\"，但原文\nlanguage is a sensory-motor delight rather than the route to dull meaning 明说对婴\n儿而言语言是感官愉悦、而非通往词义的路径，正与\"专注词义\"相反。\n借用原文：language is a sensory-motor delight rather than the route to dull meaning that it often is\nfor adults", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "According to the talk, which country played a leading role in the invention and promotion of cinema?", "options": ["A) Britain.", "B) Germany.", "C) France.", "D) Japan."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） France.\n【定位】It was above all the French, followed closely by the Americans, who were the most\npassionate exporters of the new invention... In terms of artistic development, it was again the\nFrench and the Americans who took the lead.\n首先是法国人、紧随其后是美国人，他们是这项新发明最热情的输出者……在艺术发展方\n面，又是法国人和美国人起了带头作用。\n【信号】强调结构 above all + took the lead。答案句靠两处\"领先信号\"锁定：It was above all\nthe French 用 above all(首先、最重要的是)把法国顶到第一位，后面 it was again the\nFrench and the Americans who took the lead 又用 took the lead(起带头作用)再次点名\n法国。听到 above all、the most、took the lead 这类\"拔高/排第一\"的词，紧跟的对象就\n是答案。\n【替换】above all the French ↔ France.\nthe French=France\nwho were the most passionate exporters ↔ promotion of cinema\nexporters↔promotion\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到了 British(英国)，但原句是 the British and the Germans played a\nrelatively small role——明说英、德\"作用相对较小\"。选 Britain. 正是\"听到 British 就\n选\"，恰好选反，英国是\"作用小\"的反例，不是带头者。\n借用原文：the British and the Germans played a relatively small role\nB) 原词陷阱\nGermany 也在录音里出现，但同样在 the British and the Germans played a relatively\nsmall role 这句里被归为\"作用相对较小\"的一方。选 Germany. 与 A 一样是被原词勾住、却\n选了\"配角\"。\n借用原文：the British and the Germans played a relatively small role\nD) 答非所问\n录音里 Japan 出现在 helping to start cinema in China, Japan, Latin America and\nRussia——它是法国、美国\"帮助开办电影\"的对象国之一，是被带动方，根本不是\"带头发\n明与推广\"的国家。选 Japan. 属于把\"被传播地\"错当成\"传播主导者\"。\n借用原文：helping to start cinema in China, Japan, Latin America and Russia", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "According to the talk, what can we learn about the film market back then?", "options": ["A) The United States had a leading position on the world market by the start of World War I.", "B) The centre of film-making moved eastwards from Hollywood to New York.", "C) Many film industries could compete with that of Hollywood.", "D) The film markets of Russia and Japan were the only promising ones."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\nThe United States had a leading position on the world market by the start of World War I.\n【定位】By protecting their own market and pursuing a vigorous export policy, the Americans\nachieved a dominant position on the world market by the start of the First World War.\n通过保护本国市场、推行强有力的出口政策，美国在第一次世界大战开始之时就在世界市场\n上取得了主导地位。\n【信号】结论句 achieved a dominant position。答案句是第 3 段的一句明确结论：the\nAmericans achieved a dominant position on the world market by the start of the\nFirst World War——主语美国、谓语 achieved a dominant position(取得主导地位)、时\n间 by the start of the First World War(一战开始之时)三要素齐全，是一句可直接对选项的\n事实陈述句。\n【替换】the Americans achieved a dominant position ↔ The United States had a leading position\ndominant position=leading position\nby the start of the First World War ↔ by the start of World War I\nFirst World War=World War I\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音说 The centre of film-making had moved westwards, to Hollywood——制片中心\n是\"西移\"到好莱坞。选项 moved eastwards from Hollywood to New York 把方向改\n成\"东移\"、且说成\"从好莱坞到纽约\"，方向与起讫全反。它盗用了 centre of film-\nmaking、Hollywood 等原词来制造\"听过\"的错觉。\n借用原文：The centre of film-making had moved westwards, to Hollywood\nC) 与原文相反\n录音原话是 Faced with total Hollywood domination, few film industries proved\ncompetitive——\"几乎没有(few)电影业能与好莱坞竞争\"。选项 Many film industries\ncould compete 把 few(几乎没有)换成 Many(许多)，意思整个反掉。它盗用了 film\nindustries、compete、Hollywood 等原词钓人。\n借用原文：few film industries proved competitive\nD) 偷换概念\n录音讲 in Russia and in Japan the development of the cinema took place in\nconditions of commercial isolation——俄、日是在\"商业孤立\"的条件下发展电影，是被\n隔绝、不利的状态。选项 The film markets of Russia and Japan were the only\npromising ones 却说它们是\"唯一有前景的\"，把\"孤立\"硬拗成\"被看好\"，并加了原文没有的\nonly。盗用了 Russia、Japan 原词。\n借用原文：in Russia and in Japan the development of the cinema took place in conditions of\ncommercial isolation", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What is the speaker's attitude towards the film industry?", "options": ["A) Indifferent.", "B) Biased.", "C) Negative.", "D) Neutral."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Neutral.\n【定位】The history of the cinema in its first thirty years is one of major and, to this day, unparalleled\nexpansion and growth... the British and the Germans played a relatively small role... it was\nthe United States that was to become, and remain, the largest single market for films.\n电影头三十年的历史是一部规模空前、至今无可比拟的扩张与发展史……英国人和德国人作\n用相对较小……最终是美国成为并一直保持电影最大的单一市场。\n【信号】通篇客观陈述、无褒贬语气。态度题的\"信号\"不是某个词，而是全篇口吻：录音从头到尾都\n是 the British and the Germans played a relatively small role、few film industries\nproved competitive 这类摆事实、讲历史的客观陈述，全程没有出现 great、terrible、\nunfortunately、I think 等说话人个人褒贬，语气始终中立平实。\n【替换】By protecting their own market and pursuing a vigorous export policy, the Americans achieved a\ndominant position\n↔ Neutral.\n客观陈述=中立态度\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nIndifferent. 是\"漠不关心、无所谓\"，含\"懒得理会\"的意味。但说话人花大量笔墨细致梳理各\n国电影业发展(如 the Americans achieved a dominant position)，态度是认真投入的，谈\n不上\"漠不关心\"。它与 Neutral 都\"无明显情绪\"，最易混。\nB) 过度推断\nBiased. 是\"有偏见、偏袒\"。但说话人对各国一视同仁地陈述——既说 above all the\nFrench 领先，也客观指出 the British and the Germans played a relatively small role，\n没有刻意拔高或贬低某国，不存在偏袒。\nC) 与原文相反\nNegative. 是\"否定、消极\"，要选它须有明显的批评或唱衰口吻。但全篇开头反而说电影史\n是 unparalleled expansion and growth(空前的扩张与发展)，整体平实甚至略带肯定，绝\n无消极否定的语气。\n借用原文：unparalleled expansion and growth", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) philosophy\n名词\n【词性槽】限定词 this + 空 + 介词短语，作动词 put 的宾语\n【依据】this 回指开篇格言——它正是一条人生信条\nthis 回指上一句 That which does not kill us makes us stronger.，而\"杀不死你的让你更\n强\"恰是尼采式的人生信条/哲学，所以 this philosophy = 这条信条，与 put...into\npractice（付诸实践）搭配自然。\n【竞争词】G) interpretation interpretation 是\"对某物的解释\"，不能直接指代格言本身\ninterpretation 通常说 the interpretation of X（对 X 的解读），指的是\"解释、阐释\"这个动作\n或结果，而上句给出的本身就是一句箴言、不是对箴言的解读，因此 this interpretation 在指代\n上断链。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) mirrors\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 What they do online + 空 + 宾语 what they might otherwise do，缺第三人称\n单数谓语\n【依据】线上行为\"映照\"出他们本会做的线下事——主旨级动词\n本段主题是\"青少年因 mobility 被限制，转向线上社交\"，紧接着说他们在网上做的事 often\n__(27)__ what they might otherwise do if their mobility weren't so heavily __(28)__\n——即\"反映/映照\"出他们本来若能自由活动会做的事。mirror（如镜子般反映）正是此\n义，与全篇\"线上是线下的镜像\"的论证逻辑高度自洽。\n【竞争词】C) contains\ncontains 是\"包含/容纳\"，逻辑反了——线上行为不可能\"包含\"假设语境下的本会做的行为\ncontain 的宾语应是被涵盖的子集；但本句宾语 what they might otherwise do 是与线上并列\n的另一种行为，二者是\"映射\"关系不是\"包含\"关系。\nN) undermines\nundermines 是\"削弱、破坏\"，感情色彩负面，与本句中性陈述\"行为对应关系\"不合\n线上行为\"破坏\"线下本会做的行为，逻辑不通；且本段尚未进入\"批评家长\"的负面段落，N 留给\n末段 35 题更贴。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 C) —— contains 表面满足三单 + 名词从句宾语，但搞错了主宾关\n系。What they do online often __(27)__ what they might otherwise do：主宾是\"映\n照\"不是\"包含\"，contains 把映射关系错读成集合关系。防坑：动词空先给主宾关系命名\n（mirror/contain/undermine 互斥），再去词库挑词。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) constrained\n动词\n【词性槽】weren't so heavily __ ，副词后 + be 动词后 → 过去分词或形容词\n【依据】mobility being constrained = 行动受限——与上文 Locked indoors 主题闭环\n上文 Locked indoors, unable to get on their bicycles and hang out with their friends\n已经摆明孩子的行动被困；本句用虚拟语气说\"若他们的行动 mobility 没被这样大幅约\n束\"，constrained（受限的）正是 mobility 这个抽象名词的招牌搭配。\n【竞争词】H) magnified magnified mobility 语义反——\"行动被放大\"在此语境完全不通\nmagnify 是\"放大、增强\"，与本段\"行动被困\"的主线完全相反；且家长式监护是限制 mobility\n而非扩大它。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) explore\n动词\n【词性槽】the freedom to + 空 + 名词宾语，to 后必须是动词原形\n【依据】explore one's identity 是青少年成长经典短语——探索自我与世界\n本句讲青少年要\"自己的地盘\" teens need a place to call their own，紧跟着说他们想要\nthe freedom to explore their identity and the world around them（探索自我身份与周\n围世界的自由）——这恰是青少年自我认同建构的核心命题，与全文\"放手让孩子成长\"的主\n题完全合拍。\n【竞争词】E) influence influence the world 虽可搭，但搭 identity 别扭，且与\"自由\"语义错位\ninfluence their identity 不是地道表达——身份是被探索/塑造的，不是被\"影响\"的；且\"想要\n影响身份和世界\"在 freedom（自由）一词下显得过于宏大、与本段\"建身份、找归属\"的语境\n不齐。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) sneaking\n动词\n【词性槽】Instead of + 空 + 副词 out，介词后接动名词\n【依据】sneak out = 偷溜出门，呼应上段\"骑车出门\"对照\"上网\"\n上文 Locked indoors, unable to get on their bicycles and hang out with their friends\n与下文 they jump online 构成\"线下偷溜出去玩 vs 线上社交\"的对照——孩子无法实地出\n门，于是上网代替；sneak out（偷偷溜出去）正是青少年瞒着家长出门的经典动作，与本\n段\"自由 vs 直升机式家长\"的主题严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】M) sticking stick out = 伸出/突出，不指\"出门\"\nstick out 多表\"伸出去、显眼\"，与\"出门玩\"的语义无关；与上文 hang out with their friends\n的\"出门会友\"也对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) potential\n形容词\n【词性槽】all the + 空 + 复数名词 dangers，作前置定语\n【依据】家长\"想象\"的是尚未发生、潜在的危险\n本句 parents have projected their fears onto the Internet, imagining all the __(31)__\ndangers that youth might face 中，动词 imagining 和情态 might face 双双暗示这些危\n险\"可能存在但未必发生\"，与 potential dangers（潜在的危险）逻辑严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】H) magnified magnified 偏\"被夸大的\"，与本句客观陈述\"想象潜在危险\"的中性语气不合\nmagnified dangers 暗含作者评价\"这些危险被家长夸大了\"，但本句作者只是描述家长在脑补，\n未直接评价是否夸大；且 might face 已把\"潜在\"语义占满，再叠加\"被放大\"逻辑冗余。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— magnified 词义看着也沾边，会被\"家长夸大\"的常识引诱。\nimagining all the __(31)__ dangers that youth might face：might face 已经把\"潜在\"语\n义占满，再加 magnified 逻辑冗余、且作者并未在此句直接做\"夸大\"评价。防坑：抓\nmight/imagining 这类情态/猜想信号 → potential。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) violent\n形容词\n【词性槽】from + 空 + 名词 strangers，作前置定语\n【依据】与 cruel peers 并列负面色彩——暴力的陌生人\n本段在罗列家长对网络的恐惧 imagining all the __(31)__ dangers that youth might\nface，并列项 cruel peers（残忍的同伴）已定下\"危险\"基调，violent strangers（暴力的\n陌生人）与之色彩齐平，自然构成家长担心的几种威胁之一。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) interacting\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 of + 空 + with，介词后只能接动名词\n【依据】interact with 是固定搭配——与他人互动\ninteract 天然搭 with（与某人互动）；本句讲青少年应学会\"协商公共生活、应对与他人交\n往的风险\"，the risks of interacting with others（与他人互动的风险）语义贴切，与上文\nsocialize with their peers 主题一脉相承。\n【竞争词】L) sneaking sneaking 常搭 out/in/around，不搭 with others\nsneak out（偷溜出去）是固定搭配（见 30 题），但 sneak with sb. 不是英语习惯表达；且\"与\n他人偷偷摸摸\"与本段讨论\"公共生活互动风险\"的主题完全不沾。\nM) sticking stick with 虽是搭配，但义为\"坚持/不离开\"，与\"risks\"语义冲突\nstick with sb. 多表\"和某人不分开/坚持站在某人一边\"，并不暗含可冒出 risks 的不确定互动；\n本句要的是\"社交互动\"的中性义，正中 interacting with others。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) assess\n动词\n【词性槽】并列动词原形——the skills they need to manage..., __(34)__, and get help\n【依据】assess risks = 评估风险，是固定搭配\nassess risks（评估风险）是英语里最常见的搭配之一；本段讲青少年应学会 manage\nsocial situations、assess risks、get help——三动作层层递进：判断局面、评估风险、寻\n求帮助，逻辑链条完美。\n【竞争词】C) contains\ncontain risks 偏\"遏制风险\"，与 get help 的层次断裂；且形态是第三人称单数，不能与原\n形 manage/get 并列\ncontain 作\"遏制\"时确实可搭 risks，但语义偏向\"控制住已发生的风险\"，与本段教青少年\"识别\n和应对\"的成长教育语义不齐；且 contains 是第三人称单数，与并列项 manage/get 形态不一\n致，语法上直接出局。\nE) influence influence risks 不是英语习惯搭配\ninfluence 多搭 people/decision/outcome，搭 risks 既不固定也语义不顺；本段也未谈\"影响\n风险\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) assess", "B) constrained", "C) contains", "D) explore", "E) influence", "F) interacting", "G) interpretation", "H) magnified", "I) mirrors", "J) philosophy", "K) potential", "L) sneaking", "M) sticking", "N) undermines", "O) violent"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) undermines\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 it（单数）+ 空 + 宾语 the learning，缺第三人称单数谓语\n【依据】但实际上\"保护\"在暗中削弱孩子的学习历练——与 but 的转折正负相对\n前半句说\"保护看起来像在做对的事\"，but 之后必须给出反面评价；undermine the\nlearning（暗中削弱学习历练）正是作者的核心论点——过度保护剥夺了孩子在现实中练习\n独立的机会，与 overview 主旨完全相合。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "In theory, free competition is supposed to reduce the margin of profits to the minimum.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】完美竞争与利润趋零\n【定位】Perfect competition should drive profits to zero, at least theoretically, but we have\nmonopolies making persistently high profits.\n至少在理论上，完美竞争应当把利润压到零，但我们这里却有垄断在持续赚取高额利润。\n【改写】In theory ↔ at least theoretically\n状语同义\nfree competition ↔ Perfect competition\n术语近义\nreduce the margin of profits to the minimum ↔ drive profits to zero\n程度降级", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The United States is now characterized by a great division between the rich and the poor.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】美国贫富大分裂的现状描述\n【定位】A country that experienced shared growth after World WarⅡ began to tear apart, so much so\nthat when the Great Recession hit in late 2007, one could no longer ignore the division that\nhad come to define the American economic landscape.\n一个二战后曾共享增长的国家开始撕裂，撕裂到当 2007 年末大衰退来临时，人们再也无法\n忽视那条已经定义了美国经济版图的分裂线。\n【改写】The United States is now characterized by a great division between the rich and the poor ↔\nthe division that had come to define the American economic landscape\ndefine 与 be characterized by 互换\n【辨邻】 I I段说Our divisions are deep，看似贴近\"贫富大分裂\"，但它只从顶层一侧讲分化（顶\n层免疫于底层困境、把特权当自然权利），并未做\"美国整体被分裂所定义\"的总览陈\n述，覆盖范围只是\"分裂之深\"的一面。\nOur divisions are deep. Economic and geographic segregation has immunized those at the top from\nthe problems of those down below.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "America lacked the incentive to care for the majority of its citizens as it found no rival for its economic model.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无对手便无照顾多数人的动力\n【定位】As America triumphed in the Cold War, there didn't seem to be a real competitor to our\neconomic model. Without this international competition, we no longer had to show that our\nsystem could deliver for most of our citizens.\n随着美国在冷战中获胜，似乎没有了与我们经济模式真正竞争的对手。没有了这种国际竞\n争，我们也就不再需要证明这套制度能让大多数公民受益。\n【改写】found no rival for its economic model ↔\nthere didn't seem to be a real competitor to our economic model\nrival 同义 competitor\nlacked the incentive to care for the majority of its citizens ↔\nwe no longer had to show that our system could deliver for most of our citizens\n失去动力 ↔ 不再需要证明", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The wealthy top have come to take privileges for granted.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】顶层把特权当理所当然\n【定位】Like the kings of ancient times, they have come to perceive their privileged positions\nessentially as a natural right.\n如同古代的国王，他们已经把自己的优越地位本质上视作一种天然的权利。\n【改写】The wealthy top ↔ those at the top\n省略与显化\ntake privileges for granted ↔ perceive their privileged positions essentially as a natural right\n习语 ↔ 展开义\n【辨邻】 J J段也讲顶层富人，但落点是\"财富在避税天堂积累\"\"增长流向塔尖\"这种行为与数据，\n并没有出现\"把特权视为天然/理所当然\"的心态描写。\nThe true test of an economy is not how much wealth its princes can accumulate in tax havens, but\nhow well off the typical citizen is.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Many examples show the basic laws of imperial capitalism no longer apply in present-day America.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】帝国资本主义规律不再适用\n【定位】The dynamics of the imperial capitalism of the 19th century needn't apply in the democracies\nof the 21st.\n19 世纪帝国资本主义的那套运行规律，未必适用于 21 世纪的民主国家。\n【改写】Many examples show ↔ has presented a wide range of examples\nmany ↔ a wide range of\nthe basic laws of imperial capitalism no longer apply in present-day America ↔\nThe dynamics of the imperial capitalism of the 19th century needn't apply in the democracies\nof the 21st\ndynamics ↔ basic laws", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "The author suggests a return to the true spirit of the market.", "options": null, "answer": "P", "analysis": "【答案】P 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】回归真正的市场精神\n【定位】Making markets act like markets would be a good place to start.\n让市场真的发挥市场的作用，将是一个好的开始。\n【改写】The author suggests a return to the true spirit of the market ↔\nMaking markets act like markets would be a good place to start\nact like markets ↔ true spirit of the market\n【辨邻】 Q Q段也是作者给建议的段，但落点是If we spent more on education, health and\ninfrastructure，讲的是公共投入而不是\"让市场像市场\"，与陈述句的市场主张落点\n不同。\nIf we spent more on education, health and infrastructure, we would strengthen our economy, now\nand in the future.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "A quarter of the world's prisoner population is in America.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】四分之一世界囚犯在美国\n【定位】In the eyes of the rest of the world and a significant part of its own population, mass\nimprisonment has come to define America—a country, it bears repeating, with about 5\npercent of the world's population but around a fourth of the world's prisoners.\n在世界其他国家以及美国相当一部分本国民众眼中，大规模监禁已经成为美国的标签——这\n个国家，值得反复强调，拥有世界约 5%的人口，却占据世界约四分之一的囚犯。\n【改写】A quarter of the world's prisoner population is in America ↔\nabout 5 percent of the world's population but around a fourth of the world's prisoners\nquarter 与 a fourth 同值", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Government regulation in America went from one extreme to the other in the past two decades.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】政府监管从一极荡到另一极\n【定位】The pendulum swung from much too much government there to much too little here.\nCorporate interests argued for getting rid of regulations, even when those regulations had\ndone so much to protect and improve our environment, our safety, our health and the\neconomy itself.\n钟摆从那边过多政府的一端，荡到了这边政府过少的一端。企业利益主张取消各种监管，哪\n怕这些监管曾大力保护并改善了我们的环境、安全、健康乃至经济本身。\n【改写】Government regulation in America ↔ Corporate interests argued for getting rid of regulations\nregulation 直接对应\nfrom one extreme to the other ↔ from much too much government there to much too little here\n钟摆比喻 ↔ 极端抽象\n【辨邻】 G G段讲bankers, among the strongest advocates of laissez-faire economics, were\nonly too willing to accept hundreds of billions of dollars from the\ngovernment，强调的是嘴上自由放任、实际接受政府救助的虚伪，并未给出\"从过度\n管到过度放任\"这种两极摆动描写。\nThe bankers, among the strongest advocates of laissez-faire economics, were only too willing to\naccept hundreds of billions of dollars from the government in the aid programs", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Justice has become so expensive that only a small number of people like corporate executives can afford it.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】正义昂贵到只有企业高管买得起\n【定位】Justice has become a commodity, affordable to only a few. While Wall Street executives used\ntheir expensive lawyers to ensure that their ranks were not held accountable for the misdeeds\nthat the crisis in 2008 so graphically revealed, the banks abused our legal system to foreclose\non mortgages and eject tenants, some of whom did not even owe money.\n正义已经变成一种商品，只有少数人买得起。华尔街高管用他们昂贵的律师确保自己不必为\n2008 年金融危机中暴露的劣行担责，而银行则滥用法律体系强行收回房贷、驱赶租户，其\n中有些人甚至并不欠款。\n【改写】Justice has become so expensive that only a small number of people ↔\nJustice has become a commodity, affordable to only a few\n商品 ↔ 贵到只有少数人买得起\ncorporate executives ↔ Wall Street executives\n上位词替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "No country in the world so far has been able to provide completely equal opportunities for all.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无任何国家做到完全机会平等\n【定位】Of course, no country has ever come close to providing complete equality of opportunity.\n当然，没有任何一个国家曾接近做到完全的机会平等。\n【改写】No country in the world so far has been able to provide completely equal opportunities for all ↔\nno country has ever come close to providing complete equality of opportunity\n全称否定逐项对应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "How do environmentalist opponents view GM foods according to the passage?", "options": ["A) They will eventually ruin agriculture and the environment.", "B) They are used by big businesses to monopolize agriculture.", "C) They have proved potentially harmful to consumers' health.", "D) They pose a tremendous threat to current farming practice."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How do environmentalist opponents view GM foods according to the passage\n的句式是 How + 主体 + view + 对象，问的是某方对 GM 的具体看法，且明确说\naccording to the passage，要求按原文复述，不需要跨段整合。\n【拆句】Q46 是一个标准 How 引导的特殊疑问句，主语 environmentalist opponents，谓语\nview，宾语 GM foods，状语 according to the passage。无从句、无否定、无比较，结\n构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】To environmentalist opponents, GM foods are simply evil, an understudied, possibly\nharmful tool used by big agricultural businesses to control global seed markets and crush\nlocal farmers.\n在环保派反对者看来，转基因食品简直就是恶魔——是一种研究不充分、可能有害的工具，\n被大型农业企业用来控制全球种子市场、碾压本地农民。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n选项称 GM 会「最终毁掉农业和环境」，但原文反对派的指责只到「研究不充分、可能\n有害、被巨头掌控」，并未上升到「毁掉农业」与「毁掉环境」的高度，是过度推断。\nB) ✓ 正确\nused by big agricultural businesses to control global seed markets and crush local farmers ↔\nused by big businesses to monopolize agriculture\n预判核心「巨头借 GM 垄断农业」与 used by big businesses to monopolize\nagriculture 在语义上完全等值，只是把「控制种子市场 + 碾压农民」两个并列动作压缩\n为一个动词 monopolize，是典型的同义改写。\nC) 程度范围偷改\n选项称 GM 已被「证实可能对消费者健康有害」（have proved），但原文只是\npossibly harmful tool，即「可能有害」，并未证实；possibly ≠ proved，程度被\n偷换。\nD) 无中生有\n原文中反对派的批评聚焦于「巨头垄断 + 可能有害」，未曾把 GM 描绘成「对当前农业\n造成巨大威胁」；tremendous threat 与 current farming practice 在反对派陈述里找\n不到对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author say is vital to solving the controversy between the two sides of the debate?", "options": ["A) Breaking the GM food monopoly.", "B) More friendly exchange of ideas.", "C) Regulating GM food production.", "D) More scientific research on GM crops."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say is vital to solving the controversy 是 What + author\n+ say 结构，问的是作者明说的某个具体方案，作者会用陈述句直接给出。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author say <is vital to solving the controversy>，What 既是 say\n的宾语，也是后置 is vital 的主语，相当于「作者说【什么】是解决双方争议的关键」。to\nsolving 中 to 是介词，后接动名词；考生若错把 to 当不定式 to + 原形，会读不顺，注意\n是「对于解决」之意。\n【定位】But science is exactly what we need more of when it comes to GM foods, which is why I was\nhappy to see Nature devote a special series of articles to the GM food controversy.\n但说到转基因食品，我们最需要的恰恰是更多的科学（研究）——这也是我乐于看到《自\n然》杂志专门为转基因食品争议做了一组文章的原因。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n「打破 GM 食品垄断」固然贴近反对派批评，但原文给出的解法是「更多科学」，并未\n把「打破垄断」当作解决双方争议的钥匙，因而与定位句不对应。\nB) 无中生有\n原文谈到双方对立、谈到需要更多科学，从未提及「更友善的观点交流」；friendly\nexchange of ideas 在第二段无任何原文支撑。\nC) 偷换概念\nRegulating GM food production（监管 GM 食品生产）与原文 science is what we\nneed more of 不是同一范畴——「监管」是政策行政手段，「科学研究」是认知手\n段；原文只谈后者。\nD) ✓ 正确\nscience is exactly what we need more of when it comes to GM foods ↔\nMore scientific research on GM crops\n预判核心「更多科学研究」与 More scientific research on GM crops 字面一对一对\n应，无引申、无超出原文。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is the main point of the Nature articles?", "options": ["A) Feeding the growing population makes it imperative to develop GM crops.", "B) Popularizing GM technology will help it to live up to its initial promises.", "C) Measures should be taken to ensure the safety of GM foods.", "D) Both supporters and opponents should make compromises."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干问的是 the Nature articles 这一具体对象的结论，而不是全文主旨；找到 Nature 出现\n的句子，附近便有作者转述其结论。\n【拆句】Q48 主干极简，What + be + main point of the Nature articles，问「Nature 这组文章\n的主要观点是什么」。无嵌套，无陷阱。\n【定位】The conclusion: while GM crops haven't yet realized their initial promise and have been\ndominated by agricultural businesses, there is reason to continue to use and develop them to\nhelp meet the enormous challenge of sustainably feeding a growing planet.\n结论是：虽然转基因作物尚未兑现最初的承诺、且一直被农业企业所主导，但仍有理由继续\n使用和开发它们，以帮助应对「可持续地养活不断增长的星球（人口）」这一巨大挑战。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nto help meet the enormous challenge of sustainably feeding a growing planet ↔\nFeeding the growing population\nthere is reason to continue to use and develop them ↔\nmakes it imperative to develop GM crops\n预判「为应对养活人口挑战 → 继续开发 GM」与 Feeding the growing population\nmakes it imperative to develop GM crops 在「因为人口压力 → 所以要开发 GM」的\n逻辑链上完全对齐，是定位句结论的标准同义改写。\nB) 无中生有 + 因果倒置\n选项称「推广 GM 技术将帮助它兑现最初承诺」，但原文先承认 GM crops haven't yet\nrealized their initial promise，并未把「推广」作为「兑现承诺」的手段；让 GM 兑现\n承诺并非 Nature 文章的结论方向。\nC) 答非所问\nNature 系列文章的结论谈的是「为何要继续用 GM」，并非「如何确保 GM 食品安\n全」；ensure the safety 在结论句里没有立足点。\nD) 无中生有\nNature 文章的结论根本没谈「双方妥协」，原文只在结论里给出「为养活人口而继续开\n发 GM」的判断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is the author's view on the solution to agricultural problems?", "options": ["A) It has to depend more and more on GM technology.", "B) It is vital to the sustainable development of human society.", "C) GM crops should be allowed until better alternatives are found.", "D) Whatever is useful to boost farming efficiency should be encouraged."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the author's view on the solution to agricultural problems 问的是作者对\n一个具体方案话题的看法，且未含 infer / imply，原文会以陈述句明示。\n【拆句】Q49 主干 What + be + the author's view on X，question on the solution to\nagricultural problems 是一连串名词后置修饰，view on… 表示「对……的看法」。无从\n句、无否定，结构平铺直叙。\n【定位】But anything that can increase farming efficiency—the amount of crops we can produce per\nacre of land—will be extremely useful.\n但是，凡是能提高农业效率——即我们能在每英亩土地上生产的作物量——的东西都将非常\n有用。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文段首即说 That doesn't mean GM crops are perfect, or a one-size-fits-all\nsolution，明确否认「越来越依赖 GM」；选项 depend more and more on GM\ntechnology 恰与作者立场相反。\nB) 答非所问\n「农业问题的解决方案对人类社会可持续发展至关重要」固然听起来不错，但题干问的\n是「作者对解决方案本身的看法」，不是问「解决方案的价值定位」；范畴错位。\nC) 无中生有\n原文未提「GM 应被允许直到更好的替代品出现」这种时限性立场；作者只说效率优\n先、不把 GM 当万能解，但也没说「找到替代品就停用 GM」。\nD) ✓ 正确\nanything that can increase farming efficiency…will be extremely useful ↔\nWhatever is useful to boost farming efficiency should be encouraged\n预判「凡能提效率皆可取」与 Whatever is useful to boost farming efficiency should\nbe encouraged 在「全称引导 + 提升效率 + 价值肯定」三要素上完全对齐，是定位句\n的标准同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author think of the ongoing debate around GM crops?", "options": ["A) It arises out of ignorance of and prejudice against new science.", "B) It distracts the public attention from other key issues of the world.", "C) Efforts spent on it should be turned to more urgent issues of agriculture.", "D) Neither side is likely to give in until more convincing evidence is found."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author think of the ongoing debate around GM crops 问的是作\n者对一个具体对象「the ongoing debate」的态度评价，第四段有显性态度句直接对应。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 What + does the author think of X，X 为 the ongoing debate around GM\ncrops，think of 是「认为」「评价」之意，around 这里相当于 about / surrounding，表\n示「围绕 GM 作物的争论」。无从句陷阱。\n【定位】Most of all, though, I wish a tenth of the energy that's spent endlessly debating GM crops was\nfocused on those more pressing challenges for global agriculture. There are much bigger\nbattles to fight.\n尤其是——我希望把现在花在没完没了地辩论转基因作物上的精力的哪怕十分之一，转移到\n全球农业那些更紧迫的挑战上。还有更大的仗要打。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n作者尾段的态度是「争论占去精力」，并未把双方争论的根源归于「对新科学的无知与\n偏见」；ignorance 与 prejudice 在尾段毫无对应词。\nB) 范围扩大（偷换概念）\n选项称争论「分散了公众对世界其他关键问题的注意力」，但作者尾段的精力转移诉求\n是针对「全球农业更紧迫的问题」，不是「世界其他关键问题」；范围被偷换扩大。\nC) ✓ 正确\nI wish a tenth of the energy that's spent endlessly debating GM crops was focused on those more\npressing challenges for global agriculture\n↔ Efforts spent on it should be turned to more urgent issues of agriculture\n预判「争论占去精力本应他用」与 Efforts spent on it should be turned to more\nurgent issues of agriculture 在「精力转移诉求」「更紧迫的农业问题」两点上一对一\n对齐，是态度句的标准同义改写。\nD) 无中生有\n「双方都不会让步直到有更有力证据出现」是常见的争论评价，但作者尾段未提任何\n「让步 / 不让步 / 证据」相关内容；作者关心的是精力的转移方向，而不是争论双方何\n时让步。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What are students obliged to do under early decision?", "options": ["A) Look into a lot of schools before they apply.", "B) Attend the school once they are admitted.", "C) Think twice before they accept the offer.", "D) Consult the current students and alumni."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What are students obliged to do under early decision? 直接问规则条款里的硬性要求,\n属于事实细节而非推断或主旨。\n【拆句】What are students obliged to do under early decision? 是 What 引导的特殊疑问\n句,under early decision 是状语,限定动作发生的制度背景;be obliged to do 意为\"有义务\n做\",属于义务情态结构,结构简单无陷阱。\n【定位】Early decision—you apply to one school? and admission is binding—seems like a great\nchoice for nervous applicants.\n提前录取——你只申请一所学校,而且一旦录取就具有约束力——对紧张的申请者来说似乎\n是个绝佳选择。\n【选项】A) 答非所问识别\nLook into a lot of schools before they apply. 描述申请前的考察动作;但原文要求的\n是\"只申一所/录取必去\",并未规定申请前必须考察众多学校。\nB) ✓ 正确\nadmission is binding ↔ Attend the school once they are admitted.\n预判\"录取后必须就读\"与 Attend the school once they are admitted. 字面同义,\n选 B。\nC) 无中生有识别\nThink twice before they accept the offer. 听似合理,但原文并未将\"接受 offer 前再三\n考虑\"列为 early decision 的硬性义务;相反,binding 恰恰意味着无再三斟酌的余地。\nD) 张冠李戴识别\nConsult the current students and alumni. 与 speak to current students and\nalumni(校友) 表面相似,但原文是说\"常规录取下学生本可咨询\",并非 early decision 下学\n生的义务。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why do schools offer early decision?", "options": ["A) To make sure they get qualified students.", "B) To avoid competition with other colleges.", "C) To provide more opportunities for applicants.", "D) To save students the agony of choosing a school."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why do schools offer early decision? 询问的是原文明示的校方动机,不要求推断,属于细\n节题。\n【拆句】Why do schools offer early decision? 是 Why 引导的特殊疑问句,主语 schools、谓语\noffer,结构简单;offer 此处意为\"提供/开放(某项制度)\",并非\"赠送\"。\n【定位】Early decision, since it's binding, allows schools to fill their classes with qualified students; it\nallows admissions committees to select the students that are in particular demand for their\ncollege and know those students will come.\n提前录取因为具有约束力,使学校能用合格学生填满班级;它使招生委员会能选到本校特别需\n要的学生,且确知这些学生会来。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nfill their classes with qualified students ↔ To make sure they get qualified students.\n预判\"确保招到合格学生\"与 To make sure they get qualified students. 字面同义,\n选 A。\nB) 无中生有识别\nTo avoid competition with other colleges. 在原文中无对应表述;原文只说提高 yield\nrate 和挑选 in particular demand 的学生,从未提到\"避免与其他学校竞争\"。\nC) 与原文相反识别\nTo provide more opportunities for applicants. 把校方动机误读为\"为申请者提供更多\n机会\";但原文整段在讲学校的好处,而第 1 段早已挑明 schools have hidden motives,提\n示 early decision 并非为申请者多设机会。\nD) 张冠李戴识别\nTo save students the agony of choosing a school. 利用了 done with the whole\nagonizing process by December 这一表面信息,但原文是描述学生角度的\"看似省事\",\n并非校方设立 early decision 的动机。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is said to be the problem with early decision for students?", "options": ["A) It makes their application process more complicated.", "B) It places too high a demand on their research ability.", "C) It allows them little time to make informed decisions.", "D) It exerts much more psychological pressure on them."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is said to be the problem with early decision for students? 用 is said to be,提示\n原文已明说,属于事实细节题。\n【拆句】What is said to be the problem with early decision for students? 是 What 引导的疑问\n句,is said to be 是被动语态,提示\"被陈述的/原文中提到的\",for students 限定弊端对象。\n【定位】The problem is that this process effectively shortens the window of time students have to\nmake one of the most important decisions of their lives up to that point.\n问题在于,这个流程实际上压缩了学生做出迄今人生中最重要决定之一的时间窗。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反识别\nIt makes their application process more complicated. 在原文无对应;相反,首段说\nearly decision 让人 done with the whole agonizing process by December,流程上更\n简单而非更复杂。\nB) 偷换概念识别\nIt places too high a demand on their research ability. 在原文无对应;原文谈的是 \"研\n究的时间\" 被压缩,不是 \"研究能力\" 被要求过高。\nC) ✓ 正确\nshortens the window of time students have to make one of the most important decisions ↔\nIt allows them little time to make informed decisions.\nmake a more informed decision ↔ make informed decisions\n预判\"压缩深思熟虑时间\"在 C 同时由 little time 与 informed decisions 两端承接,\n选 C。\nD) 过度推断识别\nIt exerts much more psychological pressure on them. 利用首段 nervous\napplicants 制造干扰;但首段是说 early decision 反而看上去让人省心,原文未说弊端\n是\"加大心理压力\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "Why are some people opposed to early decision?", "options": ["A) It interferes with students' learning in high school.", "B) It is biased against students at ordinary high schools.", "C) It causes unnecessary confusion among college applicants.", "D) It places students from lower-income families at a disadvantage."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why are some people opposed to early decision? 询问原文已陈述的反对方理由,不需要\n推断,属细节题。\n【拆句】Why are some people opposed to early decision? Why 疑问句,be opposed to 是固定\n搭配,意为\"反对\";some people 为不定代词主语,弱化为引述他人立场的信号。\n【定位】In this regard, there's an argument against early decision, as students from lower-income\nfamilies are far less likely to have the admissions know-how to navigate the often confusing\nearly deadlines.\n在这点上,存在反对提前录取的论点,因为来自低收入家庭的学生远不太可能掌握应对那些常\n令人困惑的提前申请截止日期的招生门道。\n【选项】A) 无中生有识别\nIt interferes with students' learning in high school. 在原文无任何对应表述;反对方理\n由聚焦于\"申请门道\",不涉及对在校学习的干扰。\nB) 偷换概念识别\nIt is biased against students at ordinary high schools. 把原文 lower-income\nfamilies(家庭收入)偷换成 ordinary high schools(普通高中),概念悄然偏移。\nC) 以偏概全识别\nIt causes unnecessary confusion among college applicants. 利用了原文 often\nconfusing early deadlines 中的 confusing,但原文是说截止日期对低收入家庭学生造成\n困惑,并非\"在所有申请者中引发不必要的混乱\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nstudents from lower-income families are far less likely to have the admissions know-how ↔\nIt places students from lower-income families at a disadvantage.\nthat advantage goes largely to students who already have numerous advantages ↔\nat a disadvantage\n预判\"低收入家庭学生处不利\"与 It places students from lower-income families at a\ndisadvantage. 字面同义,选 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author advise college applicants to do?", "options": ["A) Refrain from competing with students from privileged families.", "B) Avoid choosing early decision unless they are fully prepared.", "C) Find sufficient information about their favorite schools.", "D) Look beyond the few supposedly thrilling options."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author advise college applicants to do? 问作者给出的明确建议,通常对\n应末段总结性建议句,属细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author advise college applicants to do? What 引导的疑问句,advise sb\nto do 是固定搭配,意为\"建议某人做某事\",college applicants 是 advise 的宾语,to do 是不\n定式作宾补。\n【定位】But for students who haven't yet done enough research, or who are still constantly changing\ntheir minds on favorite schools, the early-decision system needlessly and prematurely\nnarrows the field of possibility just at a time when students should be opening themselves to a\nwhole range of thrilling options.\n但对那些尚未做足研究、或心仪学校仍在不断变化的学生而言,提前录取制度在他们本应向各\n种激动人心的选择敞开自我的时刻,毫无必要且过早地收窄了可能性。\n【选项】A) 过度推断识别\nRefrain from competing with students from privileged families. 利用了第 5 段关于\nprivileged students 的内容,但作者的态度是制度本身有问题,并未建议学生\"避免与他们\n竞争\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nBut for students who haven't yet done enough research ↔ unless they are fully prepared\nneedlessly and prematurely narrows the field of possibility ↔ Avoid choosing early decision\n预判\"未充分准备别申早\"在 B 由 Avoid choosing early decision 与 unless they are\nfully prepared 同时承接,选 B。\nC) 答非所问识别\nFind sufficient information about their favorite schools. 描述的是申请前的一般性行\n动,且 favorite schools 与原文 still constantly changing their minds on favorite\nschools 字面相似;但作者本题的具体建议是\"研究不足时不要申 early\",而非笼统地\"去了\n解 favorite schools\"。\nD) 与原文相反识别\nLook beyond the few supposedly thrilling options. 利用了 thrilling options 字眼,但\n原文是说学生本应 opening themselves to a whole range of thrilling options\n——\"敞开拥抱大量精彩选项\",而非\"越过那些据说精彩的选项去找别的\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
